-- postpartum week 79: Eating well when your toddler is on antibiotics
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   79,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler is on antibiotics',
   '"The medicine is doing the real work quietly in the background — food''s job this week is just to make the days a little easier to get through."',
   'Antibiotic courses are one of the most common medicines given in early childhood — for ear infections, strep throat, urinary tract infections and similar. Antibiotics can cause nausea, reduced appetite, abdominal discomfort, rash or loose stools. The likelihood, timing and duration depend on the particular medicine and the child. Many reactions are mild, but contact the prescriber if a side effect is persistent, worsening or concerning. There''s no need to restrict your child''s diet during a course — keep offering their regular food, including dairy, fruit and vegetables, unless their doctor has said otherwise for a specific reason. If loose stools do develop, continue the normal diet and offer usual fluids; if diarrhoea becomes frequent or watery, or your child shows signs of dehydration, ask the paediatrician about a commercially prepared oral rehydration solution rather than relying on water alone. Don''t give anti-diarrhoeal medicine unless a doctor has specifically approved it for your child. Plain, unsweetened, pasteurised yogurt or curd can remain part of your child''s normal diet if tolerated. Some specific probiotic strains and products have shown possible benefit for antibiotic-associated diarrhoea, but effects vary by strain, dose and child — ordinary yogurt is food, not a guaranteed prevention or treatment. Ask the paediatrician before using a probiotic supplement, particularly for a child with reduced immunity, a central line, serious illness or other complex medical needs. Most side effects settle on their own once the course ends and don''t undo your child''s overall nutrition over a short week or two. What''s worth a call to your paediatrician: any new rash, hives or facial/throat swelling, difficulty breathing, frequent or worsening watery diarrhoea, blood in the stool, fever, dehydration, or diarrhoea that continues after the course — more on each of these later this week. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Anywhere the menu mentions "live cultures," a plain, unsweetened, pasteurised yogurt or curd with live/active cultures on the label is what''s meant, for the toddler''s own portion.',
   'If your child has reacted to an antibiotic before: tell the prescribing doctor before this course starts, even if the earlier reaction seemed mild — they may choose a different medicine or want to watch more closely this time. If your child has an ongoing health condition or a weakened immune system: ask their paediatrician before adding a probiotic supplement (food sources like plain yogurt are generally fine) — the extra caution is specifically for children with more complex health needs, not typically-developing toddlers. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to complicate the week. Following the prescription as directed, keeping meals mostly normal, and watching for the specific signs worth a call is really all there is to it.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A course of antibiotics is one of the most common medicines a toddler will take in these early years — for an ear infection, strep throat, a urinary tract infection or something similar. Antibiotics can cause nausea, reduced appetite, abdominal discomfort, rash or loose stools. The likelihood, timing and duration depend on the particular medicine and the child. Many reactions are mild, but contact the prescriber if a side effect is persistent, worsening or concerning. This week is about making the days of the course itself easier — how to think about food and the medicine together, what to do if stools loosen, and which signs are worth a call to your paediatrician versus ordinary, expected side effects. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability, cook meat, fish and eggs all the way through, and supervise all eating closely, exactly as any other week.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked egg", "lunch_a": "Moong dal khichdi with soft-cooked bottle gourd", "lunch_b": "Moong dal khichdi with shredded chicken and soft-cooked bottle gourd", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of plain, unsweetened yogurt with live cultures", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed papaya", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chicken stew, shredded fine", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of full-fat yogurt"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Taking the medicine alongside meals", "notes": "Antibiotic instructions differ by medicine and formulation. Give each dose exactly as stated on the pharmacy label. Some can be given with or without food, while others are best given with a meal or require separation from particular foods, iron, calcium, antacids or supplements. If the label is unclear — or your child vomits after a dose — ask the pharmacist or prescriber rather than repeating, delaying or changing the dose yourself. Worth knowing: Most children do not need to avoid milk, curd or other dairy throughout an antibiotic course. However, do not use a general dairy rule for every antibiotic: follow the instructions for the particular medicine and ask the pharmacist whether any separation is needed.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semolina uttapam", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semolina uttapam with a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Tomato rice with soft-cooked carrots", "lunch_b": "Tomato rice with shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrots", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed sweet potato with a small amount of ghee", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of plain, unsweetened curd with live cultures and grated apple", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a paneer bhurji", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an egg bhurji", "still_hungry": "A slice of paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A little gut support", "notes": "Antibiotics clear out the bacteria making your child sick, but they also disturb some of the ordinary, helpful bacteria living in the gut — which is part of why loose stools are common during a course. Plain, unsweetened, pasteurised yogurt or curd can stay part of your child''s normal diet if tolerated. Some specific probiotic strains and products have shown possible benefit for antibiotic-associated diarrhoea in research, but effects vary by strain, dose and child — ordinary yogurt is food, not a guaranteed prevention or treatment. For your child: Plenty of children finish a course with no gut symptoms at all and need nothing extra. Ask the paediatrician before using a probiotic supplement, particularly for a child with reduced immunity, a central line, serious illness or other complex medical needs.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable moong dal cheela", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable moong dal cheela with a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked lauki and rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked lauki and rice with shredded chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of plain, unsweetened curd with live cultures and grated cheese", "nourishment_break_b": "A thin layer of smooth peanut butter spread on soft toast fingers, if peanut has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a lightly spiced, lower-mercury fish curry, boneless and carefully checked for fine bones", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of full-fat curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If stools loosen", "notes": "If your child does develop looser stools during the course, the current advice is to keep offering their regular diet rather than restricting it — fruit, vegetables, dal, yogurt and the usual foods are all fine, and an older approach of switching to bland starchy foods only isn''t recommended anymore, since it can prolong symptoms rather than help. Continue the normal diet and offer usual fluids; keep the antibiotic course going as prescribed unless your child''s doctor tells you otherwise. If diarrhoea becomes frequent or watery, vomiting occurs, or your child shows signs of dehydration, ask the paediatrician about a commercially prepared oral rehydration solution — it replaces lost fluid and electrolytes, which water alone does not. Worth knowing: Don''t give any anti-diarrhoeal medicine unless your child''s own doctor has specifically approved it for them — these aren''t generally advised for young children on their own. A barrier cream at nappy changes can help if the skin gets irritated from more frequent stools.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with a smooth coconut chutney, no whole nuts or seeds", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked methi and potato sabzi with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked methi and potato sabzi with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Banana slices with a small bowl of plain, unsweetened curd with live cultures", "nourishment_break_b": "Stewed apple, cooled", "dinner_a": "Bottle gourd khichdi with a small amount of ghee", "dinner_b": "Bottle gourd khichdi with shredded chicken", "still_hungry": "Mashed chickpeas with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Finishing the full course", "notes": "Give the antibiotic for the dose, timing and number of days prescribed, even once your child seems completely back to normal partway through. Do not stop early, extend the course, skip doses or use leftovers unless the prescriber changes the plan. Never save leftover doses for a future illness or share them with another child — a new illness needs its own assessment. For you: If side effects are making the course genuinely difficult — persistent vomiting, a child refusing the medicine outright, or a reaction that concerns you — call the prescribing doctor to ask about it rather than deciding alone to stop or skip doses.", "breakfast_a": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared with whole milk and grated apple", "breakfast_b": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared with whole milk and grated cheese stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and soft-cooked green beans", "lunch_b": "Rice with minced mutton and soft-cooked green beans", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of plain, unsweetened yogurt with live cultures and grated carrot", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed mango", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a fully cooked mutton curry, tender and finely minced or finely shredded", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of full-fat yogurt"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to call your paediatrician", "notes": "Hives, swelling of the lips, face, tongue or throat, breathing difficulty, wheezing, collapse or marked drowsiness require emergency care. Any new rash during an antibiotic course should be reported promptly to the prescriber, who can decide whether the next dose should be given — don''t label the child allergic, or dismiss the rash as harmless, without medical advice. For your child: White patches on the tongue, gums, cheeks or roof of the mouth — especially patches that don''t wipe away easily — may be oral thrush, but milk residue and other conditions can look similar. Ask the paediatrician to assess them rather than diagnosing or treating them yourself. Seek medical advice for frequent or worsening watery diarrhoea, blood in the stool, significant abdominal pain or cramping, fever, dehydration, or diarrhoea that continues after the antibiotic course. The clinician can decide whether testing or treatment is needed.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla with grated vegetables", "breakfast_b": "Besan chilla with grated vegetables and a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked carrot and lightly mashed peas curry with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked carrot and lightly mashed peas curry with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of plain, unsweetened curd with live cultures and mashed banana", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed banana with a small amount of smooth peanut butter, if peanut has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a rajma curry", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a boneless, lower-mercury fish curry, carefully checked for fine bones", "still_hungry": "A slice of paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did anything about this course feel easier once you had a clearer sense of what was ordinary — and is there anything worth remembering for next time your child needs antibiotics?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'A short course of medicine and a few days of an off appetite can feel worrying in the moment — what would it feel like to trust that this is a small, temporary chapter, not a setback to your child''s overall nourishment?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 80: Eating well when a new sibling arrives
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   80,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when a new sibling arrives',
   '"Wanting to be fed like a baby again isn''t your toddler going backward — it''s them checking that they still fit. A little extra help at the table answers that faster than any argument would."',
   'You''re still in charge of what''s offered, roughly when, and where — that structure doesn''t need to change just because the household is stretched. What your toddler is asking for, when they want to be fed like a baby again, is your attention and reassurance, not a different diet. Offering a little temporary help, without turning it into a struggle, can reassure your toddler while you continue supporting their established feeding skills. A brief wish for more feeding help or softer food isn''t, by itself, a feeding disorder — offer reassurance while continuing to provide developmentally appropriate textures and cup practice. If bottle use, texture regression or loss of feeding skills persists, seek advice rather than assuming it will resolve automatically. Keeping mealtimes on a predictable rhythm, even a simple one, gives your toddler one steady thing to count on amid a lot of household change, and it''s fine to lean on easy, repeated meals this week rather than anything elaborate. Letting your toddler help with small, real tasks — stirring, pouring, choosing between two foods — and finding food-adjacent ways to include them in caring for the baby can go a long way toward easing the jealousy that often surfaces exactly at mealtimes. None of this needs to be perfect: a few weeks of simpler meals, some extra spoon-feeding or a messier table do not undo your child''s overall nutrition. One thing that doesn''t flex, even on the hardest days: a toddler eating solid food still needs a seated, supervised moment, so it helps to settle your toddler with their own snack before you sit down to feed the baby rather than trying to watch both at once. More on all of this through the week. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If you''re finding this transition especially hard: it''s common, and it''s worth saying out loud to your partner, a friend, or your own doctor — asking for practical help with meals or the older child is not a failure, it''s exactly what this stretch calls for. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be perfect this week. Keeping meals simple, staying steady on what and when you offer, and giving a bit of extra closeness where you can is genuinely enough.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A new baby in the house can bring out surprisingly ''babyish'' behaviour in a toddler — wanting to be spoon-fed again after months of feeding themselves, asking for a bottle, or suddenly refusing a food they used to love. This is one of the most common, well-documented adjustments toddlers make after a sibling arrives, right alongside things like clinginess or a toileting hiccup, and it isn''t a feeding problem to fix — it''s a bid for reassurance. This week is about meeting that bid without turning food into the argument: keeping your side of the job (what, when, and where you offer) steady, while giving your toddler the attention and closeness they''re really asking for. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability and supervise all eating closely — even more deliberately than usual, since attention is genuinely divided right now.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with a small bowl of curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Rice with sambar and soft-cooked vegetables", "lunch_b": "Rice with shredded chicken and sambar", "nourishment_break_a": "Banana with a few low-salt whole-wheat crackers, free of whole seeds, nuts or hard pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Thoroughly mashed sweet corn with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a shredded chicken curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If they ask for a bottle or a puree again", "notes": "Wanting a bottle again can be an ordinary request for reassurance after a new sibling arrives. Acknowledge the wish and offer closeness — cuddling, spoon-feeding, or your child''s usual milk or water in their open or straw cup. You don''t need to restart bottle use to meet the emotional need. If a family does temporarily offer one, keep it brief and supervised, use only the child''s usual milk or plain water, never offer it in bed, and return gently to the established cup routine. Worth knowing: Whether your child feeds themselves or accepts temporary help from you is a smaller decision, and not one worth a fight over. Keep solid and pureed foods on a spoon or plate — never put cereal, puree or other solid food into a bottle.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with a mild sambar", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Khichdi with soft-cooked carrots", "lunch_b": "Khichdi with shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrots", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed banana", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a tomato-paneer bhurji", "still_hungry": "Grated or crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Keeping meals simple for you", "notes": "Feeding a toddler while a newborn needs you every couple of hours is genuinely a lot — this is a good week to lean on whatever''s easiest: a simple rotation of two or three meals you already know your child eats, food frozen in advance, or a scaled-back menu with no guilt attached. Refrigerate perishable food within two hours of cooking — within one hour in very hot conditions — in shallow, covered containers, use most cooked leftovers within three to four days, and reheat until steaming hot all the way through rather than just warm — easy shortcuts to skip when you''re exhausted, and worth protecting anyway. For you: Prepare a few snacks in advance and keep them in an adult-controlled place so they''re quick to serve. When your toddler asks for one, seat them safely and supervise the entire snack — soft ripe fruit, grated or crumbled paneer, mashed sweet potato, smooth hummus, or low-salt crackers without whole seeds, nuts or hard pieces all work well. Don''t leave ready-to-eat food somewhere your toddler could take it independently.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Rice with minced chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated cucumber", "nourishment_break_b": "Mild pasteurised cheese, finely grated or thinly sliced, with soft toast fingers", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a small amount of ghee", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Small jobs, big feelings", "notes": "A toddler who feels displaced often settles a little when they''re given something real to do — stirring a bowl you''re holding steady, pouring water from a small jug, or choosing between two vegetables for dinner. None of this needs to be elaborate, and it''s fine if it''s slower or messier than doing it yourself; that mess is often the sign that a toddler is genuinely engaged rather than just watching from the sidelines. For your child: If your child wants to feed themselves with their hands more than usual, or spills more than they used to, that''s ordinary toddler variation, more noticeable right now simply because you''re watching more closely. Include your toddler through safe jobs — choosing a cloth, bringing a bib, or sitting beside you while you feed the baby. Your toddler should never give the baby food, milk, medicine or a bottle without you directly controlling it.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked bhindi sabzi with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked bhindi sabzi with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Grated apple with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted sweet potato sticks, soft enough to mash with a fork", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an egg curry", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Together time around food", "notes": "Before you sit down to nurse or feed the baby, it can help to settle your toddler with their own seated snack and something to do nearby, rather than trying to occupy them in the middle of it — it''s easier on everyone, and it means your toddler isn''t eating unsupervised while you''re occupied. Some families find it helps to let the older child ''feed'' a favourite doll or toy alongside you, or to talk quietly about when they were a baby too. For you: If your toddler wants to be close while you feed the baby, saying something simple like \"it''s hard to wait while I feed your brother/sister\" tends to land better than asking them to be patient or grown-up about it.", "breakfast_a": "Soft dosa with a smooth mild tomato chutney, no hard pieces", "breakfast_b": "Soft dosa with a fully cooked egg stirred through", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked spinach", "lunch_b": "Rice with minced mutton and soft-cooked spinach", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated carrot", "nourishment_break_b": "Orange segments, seeds and tough membrane removed, cut into soft pieces", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma curry and mashed potato", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a tender, finely minced mutton curry, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with grated carrot"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Staying safe, and when to loop in your paediatrician", "notes": "Even on the busiest days, a toddler eating solid food still needs a seated, watched moment — this isn''t a rule that can flex just because there are two children needing you at once. It''s worth planning around it: a toddler''s snack and seat happen before the baby''s feed starts, not alongside it, so nobody''s eating unsupervised. For your child: Cut grapes and cherry or grape tomatoes lengthwise into quarters. Remove cherry stones and cut cherries into small, developmentally appropriate pieces. Don''t offer whole or chopped nuts, popcorn, hard raw vegetable pieces, or thick spoonfuls of nut or seed butter — spread nut or seed butter thinly, keep your toddler seated upright, and supervise continuously. Speak with your paediatrician if feeding regression persists or worsens; your child loses previously established chewing, swallowing or cup skills; they frequently cough, choke or gag during meals; they accept progressively fewer textures or foods; they become distressed at most meals; they lose weight; or you notice regression across several developmental areas rather than only seeking extra reassurance.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked mixed vegetable curry with khichdi", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked mixed vegetable curry with shredded chicken and khichdi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A thin layer of smooth almond butter spread on soft toast fingers, if almond has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a lower-mercury fish curry, boneless and carefully checked for fine bones", "still_hungry": "Grated or crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Was there a moment this week where a little extra help at the table said more than words could — and is there a small routine worth keeping as things settle?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'You''re feeding two children on very little sleep, and some days that will look like extra help with a meal or food made in five minutes flat. What would it feel like to let that be enough, rather than seeing it as a sign that you''re falling behind?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 81: Feeding an increasingly independent 18-month-old
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   81,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Feeding an increasingly independent 18-month-old',
   '"Your child is reaching for the spoon, the cup, and the word ''no'' all at once — mealtimes are just where that reach happens to show up first."',
   'Appetite genuinely varies day to day and meal to meal at this age, because growth has slowed a great deal since the first birthday — a big plate cleared one day and barely a few bites the next are both within the range of normal. What tends to help most is a predictable rhythm: three meals and two or three sit-down snacks, roughly two to three hours apart, offered at fairly consistent times. Self-feeding is being actively practised rather than mastered — confident finger-feeding, an eager but imperfect go at the spoon, real spillage from an open cup. All of this is on schedule, and it''s still completely normal to help by loading the spoon or offering an assisted bite. The full choking-precaution list from earlier months still applies. Even as chewing skills improve, young children may not reliably manage foods that are hard, round, sticky, tough or difficult to break apart. ''No'' is becoming a favourite word well beyond the table, and food refusal is often part of that same push for independence rather than a verdict on the meal. The steadiest response stays the same: you decide what''s offered, when, and where; your child decides whether and how much — and a bounded choice between two options can give your child a real say without opening the whole menu to negotiation. Selective eating often fluctuates during toddlerhood. A calm, consistent approach can reduce mealtime pressure, although acceptance may change gradually and every child''s timeline is different. It''s worth a word with your paediatrician if mealtimes are distressing most of the time regardless of what''s offered, only a very small handful of foods are accepted overall, or a previously-managed food or texture is suddenly refused across the board — more on the specific signs later this week. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If mealtimes have started to feel tense: it''s common for a toddler''s new independence to bring some friction to the table — stepping back from persuading or pressuring, even when it feels counterintuitive, tends to help more than holding firm. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be a bigger week than usual. A steady rhythm, room for the mess of learning, and a bit of patience with a new favourite word is genuinely enough.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Somewhere around now, appetite gets genuinely unpredictable — your child may eat well at one meal and barely touch the next, or want twice as much one day as the next. This isn''t a problem to solve; it''s a direct result of growth slowing down after the first birthday, and it means less food is actually needed than it looks like from the outside. This week is about meeting that unpredictability with steady structure rather than worry: the same offered meals and snacks, on a reliable rhythm, while your child''s own appetite decides the rest. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability and supervise all eating closely — more on textures and safety later this week.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Oats porridge with a fully cooked egg", "lunch_a": "Tomato rice with soft-cooked green beans", "lunch_b": "Tomato rice with shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans", "nourishment_break_a": "Finely grated apple with a small bowl of yogurt", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with mashed banana", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chicken curry, finely shredded", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of yogurt"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A rhythm worth keeping", "notes": "Three meals and two or three sit-down snacks, spaced roughly two to three hours apart at fairly predictable times, gives your child''s appetite something steady to work against even on days it swings wildly. It''s the timing and setting that stay consistent — not how much actually gets eaten at any one of them. Worth knowing: Water can be available between meals and snacks. Avoid continuous grazing or frequent juice and milk drinks that repeatedly replace appetite for food. If your child is breastfeeding, feeds can continue responsively alongside this meal-and-snack rhythm.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable rava idli", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable rava idli with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Moong dal khichdi with soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Moong dal khichdi with shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed sweet potato with a small bowl of yogurt", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with grated cucumber", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a paneer bhurji", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an egg bhurji", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Self-feeding, on their own timeline", "notes": "Confident finger-feeding is typical by now, and spoon use is actively being practised — genuinely practised, not yet mastered. Expect real spillage with an open cup and a fair amount of missed spoonfuls; both are exactly on schedule, not behind it. Loading the spoon for them or offering an assisted bite here and there is still completely normal help at this age. For your child: It''s fine to let a meal be slower and messier than it would be if you fed them yourself — the practice is the point, and full utensil competence isn''t expected for another year or so.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semiya upma", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semiya upma with a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked ridge gourd and moong dal with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked ridge gourd and rice with shredded chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe papaya cubes, mashed if needed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with grated cheese", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with fully cooked lower-mercury fish, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Safe textures as chewing catches up", "notes": "Your child may now manage a wide range of family foods, but chewing and swallowing skills are still developing. Food shape, size and texture therefore remain important: whole grapes and cherry tomatoes should be quartered lengthwise; nuts and popcorn avoided; smooth nut butter spread thinly; meat cooked tender and finely shredded; and cheese grated or served in thin, short strips rather than cubes. Worth knowing: This full list of precautions is recommended until around age four, not just to two — it''s a function of chewing development, not a birthday.", "breakfast_a": "Suji cheela with grated vegetables", "breakfast_b": "Suji cheela with grated vegetables and a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked cabbage and potato sabzi with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked cabbage and potato sabzi with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of yogurt with finely grated carrot", "nourishment_break_b": "A thin spread of smooth peanut butter on soft toast fingers, if peanut has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma curry, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When ''no'' shows up at the table", "notes": "Around now, ''no'' becomes a favourite word well beyond mealtimes — in dressing, in play, in pretty much everything — and food refusal is often just that same push for independence showing up at the table, not a verdict on the meal itself. The steadiest response is the one that''s worked all along: you decide what''s offered, when, and where; your child decides whether and how much. For your child: Offering a bounded choice — ''peas or carrots?'' — gives your child a real say without opening up the whole menu to negotiation. A new food may need to be offered repeatedly on different occasions before it becomes familiar, and the number of exposures varies from child to child.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with a smooth mild chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a lentil dal and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp removed completely from the fibrous pod", "lunch_b": "Rice with minced chicken and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp removed completely from the fibrous pod", "nourishment_break_a": "Grated cucumber with a small bowl of yogurt", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with mashed papaya", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an egg curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of yogurt"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When selective eating deserves extra support", "notes": "Refusing vegetables some days, throwing food, or sticking to a favourite for a stretch are all ordinary parts of this age. Selective eating often fluctuates during toddlerhood — a calm, consistent approach can reduce mealtime pressure, although acceptance may change gradually and every child''s timeline is different. It''s worth a conversation with your paediatrician if mealtimes are distressing at most meals regardless of what''s offered, your child accepts only a very small handful of foods overall, or a food or texture they used to manage is suddenly refused across the board. For your child: Frequent gagging, choking, or vomiting with food, signs of dehydration, real difficulty swallowing, or noticeable weight loss are the signals worth calling about promptly rather than waiting to see if they pass.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia with a boiled egg on the side", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia with grated cheese stirred in", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked carrot and peas curry with khichdi", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked carrot and peas curry with shredded chicken and khichdi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of yogurt with finely grated apple", "nourishment_break_b": "Grated pear with a small bowl of yogurt", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with fully cooked lower-mercury fish, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Where did independence show up at the table this week — in a good way or a frustrating one — and what''s one small thing you''d like to keep steady going forward?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Watching a child insist on doing it themself, spoon and all, is a strange mix of pride and mess — what would it feel like to let both be true at once, without needing the mess to mean something went wrong?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 82: Eating well while breastfeeding a toddler
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   82,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well while breastfeeding a toddler',
   '"Breastmilk used to be the whole meal — now nursing is one part of a much bigger table, still worth keeping for as long as it works for you and your child."',
   'Major health organisations support continued breastfeeding alongside solid foods to two years or beyond when it remains mutually desired. There is no routine medical cutoff at which breastfeeding suddenly becomes inappropriate — the decision can remain with you and your child for as long as it continues to work for both of you. Nutritionally, breastmilk keeps contributing real value well into the second year, supplying energy, nutrients and immune-supporting components — across populations it may provide roughly a third of energy needs between one and two, though the actual contribution varies considerably with nursing frequency and milk intake — even as solid food takes over as the main source of calories and variety. Both things are true at once: nursing still matters, and meals are now doing most of the work. Breastfeeding can continue to increase your own energy needs, though by how much varies with how often your child nurses, your activity level, body size and overall health — there''s no need to count calories rigidly, just eat regular, balanced meals and respond to your own hunger and fullness. Iron, calcium, iodine, choline, vitamin D and omega-3 fats are worth continued attention at this stage, and reliable vitamin B12 intake is essential if you eat no animal products — more on this later this week. Expect nursing itself to look different now — shorter sessions, fewer of them, clustered around specific moments like waking, naps or bedtime, and just as often about comfort as about food. These changes are common during toddlerhood and do not, by themselves, signal a problem or require you to make a decision about weaning. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. If you are not breastfeeding now, there is nothing you need to restart or compensate for. Use the family meals this week and skip the nursing-specific guidance.',
   'If nursing sessions feel unpredictable lately: that''s expected as they become fewer and more anchored to a few key moments in the day — it isn''t a sign of anything going wrong. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be a bigger week than usual. Eat to your own hunger, keep the same steady rhythm for your child, and let nursing be whatever it is right now — no more, no less.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re still nursing at this stage, it isn''t a holdover from infancy you forgot to phase out — it''s simply one part of how your child eats now, alongside three meals and a couple of snacks a day. Breastmilk continues to provide energy, nutrients and immune-supporting components during the second year — across populations, it may provide roughly a third of energy needs between 12 and 24 months, though the contribution varies considerably with nursing frequency and milk intake, even once solids make up most of the plate. That''s a genuine contribution, not just a formality — and it also means solids are carrying the larger share of the load, which is exactly how it should look by now. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability and supervise all eating closely, same as always.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable besan chilla", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable besan chilla with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Vegetable khichdi with a small bowl of curd", "lunch_b": "Vegetable khichdi with shredded chicken and a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed chikoo with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely diced ripe pear with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a lauki chana dal, chickpeas cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a keema curry, minced mutton fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft toast or vegetable sticks prepared appropriately for the person eating them"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Eating for you, not a formula", "notes": "Breastfeeding can continue to increase your energy needs, but the amount varies with how frequently your child nurses, your activity level, body size and overall health. There is no need to count calories rigidly: eat regular, balanced meals, respond to hunger and fullness, and seek individual guidance if you are losing weight unintentionally, feeling persistently depleted or managing a medical condition. For you: Continue paying attention to iron, calcium, iodine, choline, vitamin D and omega-3 fats. If you eat no animal products, reliable vitamin B12 intake is essential because deficiency can affect both mother and breastfed child. Discuss an appropriate B12 source and whether you need iron, iodine, vitamin D, DHA or another supplement with a qualified healthcare professional.", "breakfast_a": "Fully cooked sprouted-moong cheela", "breakfast_b": "Fully cooked sprouted-moong cheela with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rajma curry with rice, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with shredded chicken and a lightly spiced tomato gravy", "nourishment_break_a": "Finely ground almond powder stirred thoroughly into curd, if almond has already been introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "Hummus spread thinly on soft toast fingers", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a palak sabzi", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an omelette, finely chopped and fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of ground flaxseed"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where nursing fits around meals", "notes": "Nursing right before a meal can sometimes take the edge off a toddler''s appetite for solids — if you notice meals being skipped fairly often, try spacing a feed a little further from mealtimes and see whether that shifts things. There''s no single required order, though, and it''s fine to trust the pattern across a whole day rather than judging any one meal on its own. Not every nursing session this age is about hunger, and that''s completely fine — a lot of it now is comfort, reassurance, or just a quick check-in in the middle of a busy day, and that''s a legitimate reason to nurse, separate from food. Worth knowing: Frequency varies enormously between toddlers — some nurse only once or twice a day, others more — and sessions tend to be much shorter than they were in infancy, often just a couple of minutes except at naptime or bedtime.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Curd rice with soft-cooked carrot", "lunch_b": "Curd rice with shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed ripe mango with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft, seedless watermelon cut into thin, manageable pieces or lightly mashed as needed", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft rice with a lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "No expiry date on this", "notes": "There''s no medical cutoff where breastfeeding a toddler stops being appropriate — no age at which continuing suddenly becomes something to explain or defend. If you''re nursing in public with a more vocal, more mobile child now, a snack or a sip of water to bridge a few minutes, or a quiet family word for ''let''s nurse later,'' can help on days it isn''t the moment. For your child: It''s also fine to start shaping when and where nursing happens — keeping it to certain times or places while gently not offering it at others — if that suits your family better than an open-ended approach. Either way is a legitimate way to keep going. If comments from others about nursing ''this long'' come your way, you don''t owe anyone an explanation — a short, deflecting answer or simply changing the subject works just as well as a justification.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu with a smooth mild chutney", "breakfast_b": "Pesarattu with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked bottle gourd and toor dal with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked bottle gourd and rice with shredded chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked sweet potato with a little ground sesame stirred in", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates softened and blended or mashed smoothly into curd, with the stone completely removed", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a chana masala, chickpeas cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chicken curry, boneless chicken cooked until tender and finely shredded", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Biting, strikes, and telling them apart", "notes": "An occasional bite at this age is usually about teething discomfort, boredom near the end of a session, or a distracted moment — not a sign anything is wrong. Staying calm and simply ending the session without a big reaction tends to work better than a dramatic response, which some toddlers find interesting enough to repeat. For your child: A sudden refusal to nurse can happen with teething, illness, pain, stress or a change in routine and may be temporary. Avoid forcing the breast; offer calmly and watch your child''s fluids and overall wellbeing. Seek advice if refusal persists, your child appears unwell or dehydrated, or you are concerned about pain or milk supply. Weaning is often more gradual, but every child''s pattern differs.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with grated vegetables", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with grated vegetables and a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked green beans and potato sabzi with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked green beans and potato sabzi with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Very ripe, peeled guava softened and mashed, with hard seeds removed", "nourishment_break_b": "Cheese, grated or cut into thin, short strips, with soft toast fingers", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a toor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chicken kheema curry, minced and fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Good-to-know safety notes", "notes": "Many medicines are compatible with breastfeeding, but safety is specific to the medicine, dose and child. Check prescription medicines, over-the-counter products and herbal supplements with a doctor or pharmacist, or through a trusted lactation-medicine resource. Do not stop a necessary medicine or deliberately skip feeds without appropriate guidance. Alcohol and caffeine guidance hasn''t loosened either, but with fewer, more predictable nursing sessions, it''s often easier to time an occasional drink around a gap in nursing than it was in the early months. Worth knowing: Occasional shorter, skipped or differently timed sessions can be normal when nursing a toddler. Because milk production responds to milk removal, a sustained reduction in nursing may gradually reduce supply. Seek support if the change is accompanied by breast pain, concern about your child''s fluid intake or growth, or an unwanted drop in milk production. For you: If you''re worried about your milk, your child''s growth, or anything about how nursing is going, it''s always worth raising with your child''s healthcare provider — there''s no need to wait for a scheduled visit if something feels off.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked cabbage and peas curry with khichdi, peas cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked cabbage and peas curry with shredded chicken and khichdi, peas cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft toast fingers spread thinly with smooth almond butter, served with mashed banana, if almond has already been introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed ripe papaya with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp removed completely from the fibrous pod", "dinner_b": "Rice with minced mutton and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp removed completely from the fibrous pod", "still_hungry": "Finely crumbled paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. What does nursing actually give the two of you right now — food, comfort, both, something else — and does the answer change how you want to think about it going forward?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Somewhere between everyone else''s opinions about how long is ''too long,'' it''s easy to lose track of what you and your child actually think — what would it feel like to let that be the only opinion that counts here?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 83: Eating well in hot weather
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   83,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well in hot weather',
   '"Hot weather may change your appetite, but the essentials stay steady: drink water often, keep regular meals available, and pay attention to how you''re actually feeling."',
   'Use this week''s guidance whenever hot weather is actually happening where you are — if it''s currently mild or cool, continue with your usual rhythm and come back to this content the next time a heatwave rolls around. Heat changes the day for you too — smaller appetite, more sweating, and a bit more caution around food and fluids. If you''re breastfeeding, your fluid needs go up further still, so drinking proactively rather than waiting for thirst matters even more — unless you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction, in which case your prescriber''s guidance takes priority over the general advice below. This week focuses on staying ahead of hydration, eating what you can manage without forcing it, low-cooking-effort meals that are easy to keep safely stored in the heat, and the signs that heat is affecting you more than it should — including telling apart milder symptoms from a genuine heat emergency.',
   'If you manage diabetes: monitor your glucose as advised, maintain regular hydration unless you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction, and protect insulin, medicines, meters and test strips from direct heat — follow their storage instructions, and contact your care team if readings change significantly or you''re unable to eat or drink normally. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you, even with more buttermilk around in hot weather. If you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction: for a heart, kidney or other condition, follow your prescriber''s own fluid guidance in hot weather rather than the general "drink more" advice in this week. If you''re still breastfeeding: drink to thirst and keep fluids easily available — this supports how you feel, though drinking beyond that doesn''t reliably increase milk supply. A persistently reduced urine output (going noticeably less often, or dark urine), or a drop in milk output that continues for more than a day or two — especially alongside feeling unwell in the heat — is worth mentioning to your doctor. Confusion, collapse or suspected heat stroke is a medical emergency: call local emergency services immediately, move to a cooler place and begin cooling, and don''t give drinks to someone confused, unconscious or unable to swallow safely. Milder symptoms in someone alert — unusual drowsiness, a headache that won''t ease, feeling faint or unwell in the heat — still need prompt attention: move to a cooler place, sip fluids, and get medical help promptly rather than waiting to see if it passes.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, with extra care on refrigeration timing in the heat. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or your appetite is smaller than usual in the heat, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Use this week''s guidance whenever hot weather is actually happening where you are. Heat changes the day — smaller appetite, more sweating, a bit more caution needed around food and fluids, and more if you''re breastfeeding, since fluid needs go up further.", "breakfast_a": "Curd rice (1 cup, made with 150g curd) with a few curry leaves", "breakfast_b": "Chilled milk (200ml) with muesli (1 cup)", "lunch_a": "A quick pressure-cooker moong dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber salad", "lunch_b": "Tandoori chicken (100g chicken, cooked ahead and kept chilled until served) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "Watermelon cubes (chilled) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, quick one-pot) with curd", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Drink water proactively, not just when thirsty", "notes": "It''s easy to under-drink in heat, especially with a lot else going on — keep a water bottle within reach through the day and sip regularly rather than waiting to feel thirsty, unless you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction for a heart, kidney or other condition, in which case follow your prescriber''s fluid guidance instead. Water-rich foods like watermelon, cucumber and oranges are a helpful addition, not a replacement for drinking water itself.", "breakfast_a": "Chaat made with thoroughly cooked sprouts (1 cup), cooled promptly and refrigerated until served, with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold vegetable sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A quick sambar (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot salad", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "A quick masoor dal (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (quick and fully cooked) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg (kept chilled, peeled just before eating)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A smaller appetite in heat is common and usually temporary", "notes": "Eating less in hot weather is a commonly observed pattern and usually passes once the heat eases. Keep regular meals available and eat what you can manage rather than forcing a full plate — smaller, more frequent meals often sit better than three large ones in real heat.", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup, quick-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold fruit-and-curd bowl (150g curd with chopped seasonal fruit)", "lunch_a": "A quick toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "A chilled salad made with fully cooked chicken (100g), rice (1 cup cooked, cooled) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "A quick khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken, quick and fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Heat raises the food-safety stakes", "notes": "Refrigerate perishable food within two hours as usual — or within one hour once it''s been sitting above 32°C/90°F, tighter than the usual window. Low-cooking-effort dishes like curd rice, thoroughly cooked sprouts and chilled dals still need the same care once made: keep them refrigerated until served, and don''t let them sit out on a warm counter.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces, quick-steamed) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold boiled-egg-and-toast plate (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A quick masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, quick and fully cooked, kept chilled until reheated) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "A quick rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of chilled milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Replacing what the heat takes out", "notes": "Heavy sweating loses fluid and some salt together — but for ordinary heat exposure, your regular meals usually replace what''s lost, so there''s no need to routinely add extra salt to drinks. A home-style buttermilk alongside your regular meals is a reasonable, low-effort way to help with fluids. If you''re sweating heavily for a prolonged stretch — a long outdoor day, or an illness with fever in the heat — ask your doctor for individual guidance on replacing salt and fluid (CDC heat guidance).", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces, quick-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold paneer-and-vegetable sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A quick chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton, quick and fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "A quick masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s more than the heat", "notes": "Unusual drowsiness, a headache that won''t ease, or feeling faint or unwell in the heat needs prompt attention — don''t wait for hot, dry skin before taking it seriously, since heat stroke can occur even while still sweating. Confusion, collapse or suspected heat stroke is a step further than these milder symptoms and is a medical emergency. Worth knowing: Confusion, collapse or suspected heat stroke is an emergency: call local emergency services immediately, move to a cooler place and begin cooling (fan yourself, apply cool damp cloths to your skin). Do not give drinks to someone who is confused, unconscious, or unable to swallow safely. For milder heat symptoms in someone who is alert — feeling faint or unusually unwell — move to a cooler place, sip fluids, and get medical help promptly rather than waiting to see if it passes (CDC heat-illness guidance).", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces, quick-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold cucumber-and-cheese sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, quick and fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "A quick chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Have you been drinking water proactively through the heat this week, or mostly only when you noticed you were thirsty?", "breakfast_a": "Curd rice (1 cup, made with 150g curd) with a few curry leaves", "breakfast_b": "Chilled milk (200ml) with muesli (1 cup)", "lunch_a": "A quick pressure-cooker moong dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber salad", "lunch_b": "Tandoori chicken (100g chicken, cooked ahead and kept chilled until served) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "Watermelon cubes (chilled) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, quick one-pot) with curd", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat a smaller appetite in the heat as an ordinary, temporary adjustment rather than something to push through or worry about?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own comfort and safety in changing conditions deserve the same attention as anyone else''s.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 84: Eating well when you're not in charge of the kitchen
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   84,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you''re not in charge of the kitchen',
   '"A meal away from your own kitchen doesn''t need to match your usual plate — it just needs to be reasonably safe, roughly on schedule, and not something to worry over either way."',
   'Between visiting relatives, restaurants, and the odd wedding or celebration, eating away from your own kitchen is a regular part of postpartum life, not a special event to brace for. Choose whatever reasonably balanced option is actually on offer, and let the rest go. Being told what a new mother should or shouldn''t eat is a genuinely common experience, especially from well-meaning relatives — you''re allowed to politely decline advice that doesn''t fit your own needs or your doctor''s guidance. This week focuses on eating reasonably well in company, staying safe with food away from home, and letting an irregular day be exactly that — irregular, not a problem.',
   'If mealtimes away from home feel unpredictable: that''s expected — a new setting changes things for a little while, and it isn''t a sign anything is wrong. If you manage diabetes: plan for possible meal delays, continue monitoring as advised, and carry your usual fast-acting treatment for low blood glucose. Coordinate insulin or other medicines with meals according to your prescribed plan — don''t change doses on your own. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you, even at a spread where they''re plentiful. If you take levothyroxine for an underactive thyroid: continue it exactly as prescribed and keep the usual gap from iron or calcium supplements and dairy-heavy meals, even while travelling — ask your prescriber if you''re unsure how to fit that around a shifted schedule. If you have a food allergy: check ingredients and ask about cross-contact whenever you''re eating away from your own kitchen — a dish can look safe and still have been prepared alongside your trigger food. If a family gathering repeatedly leaves you feeling pressured or distressed about food specifically, that''s worth a conversation with your partner about how to handle it together, rather than something to just get through each time.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, with the usual extra care about freshness when eating out. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''re away from your own kitchen, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Between visiting relatives, restaurants, and the odd wedding or celebration, eating away from your own kitchen is a regular part of postpartum life, not a special event to brace for. Choose whatever reasonably balanced option is actually on offer, and let the rest go.", "breakfast_a": "Restaurant-style masala dosa (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A hotel-style bread omelette (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A vegetarian thali''s dal and rice (3/4 cup dal, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Butter chicken (100g chicken) from a restaurant or dhaba with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A packaged fruit-and-nut bar with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A freshly made glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) — ask for it made fresh", "dinner_a": "Dal-roti at a relative''s home (3/4 cup dal, 2 roti) with a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) — a common spread option at a gathering", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Relatives with opinions about what you should eat", "notes": "Being told what a new mother should or shouldn''t eat is a genuinely common experience, especially from well-meaning relatives — you''re allowed to politely decline advice that doesn''t fit your own needs or your doctor''s guidance, without a lengthy explanation.", "breakfast_a": "A dhaba-style stuffed paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A relative''s home-style rajma-chawal (3/4 cup rajma, 1 cup rice) with a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) from a family gathering with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A packaged roasted-chana snack pack (30g) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "A wedding-buffet-style dal-roti (3/4 cup dal, 2 roti) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (from a restaurant or gathering) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Food safety away from home follows the same basic rule", "notes": "Choose freshly cooked food served hot, or cold food kept properly chilled. Skip anything that''s clearly been sitting out at room temperature for a while, especially in warm weather — the same two-hour rule (one hour above 32°C/90°F) applies wherever you''re eating.", "breakfast_a": "Restaurant-style idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A canteen or work-cafeteria toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) freshly off the grill at a restaurant with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A whole fruit you can peel yourself with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) — ask if it''s been freshly served, not sitting out", "dinner_a": "A relative''s home-style chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) freshly served hot with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A backup snack takes the pressure off", "notes": "Keeping a familiar, genuinely portable snack on hand — for a long outing, a delayed meal, or a spread that doesn''t have much you''d want — means one less thing to worry about. Shelf-stable choices like roasted chana, nuts, dry fruit or packaged crackers travel well without any chilling; milk and curd are useful at home but aren''t realistic travel snacks unless you can keep them properly cool the whole way.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces, packed in a tiffin) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A home-packed masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) packed from home with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A shelf-stable trail mix (roasted chana, nuts and dry fruit, 30g) with a small bowl of curd (150g) eaten separately at home", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin — best had before you leave, not carried along", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A packet of roasted chana or a handful of nuts (shelf-stable, easy to carry)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A word in advance can smooth a lot", "notes": "If you know a gathering will involve pressure to eat a certain way, or opinions you''d rather not field, a quiet word beforehand with whoever''s hosting — or with your partner, about backing you up — often goes further than managing it in the moment.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A host''s home-style chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Mutton rogan josh (100g mutton) at a family celebration with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) at a restaurant or gathering with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "It doesn''t need to match your usual pattern", "notes": "A day of eating away from home rarely looks like your regular rhythm, and that''s fine — an irregular day here and there doesn''t undo your overall nutrition, and you can simply pick back up your usual pattern once you''re home.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) at a restaurant with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Was there a moment this week you''d have liked to speak up about food-related advice you didn''t ask for — and is there a simple line you could have ready for next time?", "breakfast_a": "Restaurant-style masala dosa (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A hotel-style bread omelette (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A vegetarian thali''s dal and rice (3/4 cup dal, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Butter chicken (100g chicken) from a restaurant or dhaba with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A packaged fruit-and-nut bar with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A freshly made glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) — ask for it made fresh", "dinner_a": "Dal-roti at a relative''s home (3/4 cup dal, 2 roti) with a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) — a common spread option at a gathering", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat one irregular day away from home as simply that, rather than something to make up for once you''re back?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own comfort and preferences matter in any kitchen, not just your own.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 85: Eating well during a growth spurt
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   85,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well during a growth spurt',
   '"A sudden bigger appetite is usually just your child''s hunger swinging the other way — real, ordinary, and best met with more of what''s already on the table."',
   '"Growth spurt" is a real but loosely used term. Children do grow in uneven bursts rather than perfectly smoothly, but mainstream pediatric guidance doesn''t treat a toddler "spurt" as something a parent can reliably identify in the moment — it''s far more confident about ordinary day-to-day appetite variability, which is the same variability behind an occasional smaller appetite too. A hungrier stretch is usually just that variability running the other way, and it calls for the same steady response either way. The simplest response to genuine extra hunger is more of what''s already on offer — another helping of dal, rice, roti, fruit or curd — rather than new sugary or processed food to fill the gap. Offering larger portions of familiar, healthy food is a safe way to meet a bigger appetite, since children this age are generally good at regulating how much they eat once the food itself is nutritious. A plate built around a grain, a dal or other legume, and some milk or dairy already supports what growth draws on, without needing to chase a specific number — and pairing iron-rich foods with a vitamin-C food in the same meal helps the body absorb more of that iron. A bigger appetite that comes with steady or increasing weight and energy needs nothing more than feeding it. A bigger appetite alongside weight loss, unusual thirst, frequent urination, or increasing tiredness is a different picture. Contact your child''s healthcare professional promptly — ideally the same day — for advice and assessment, rather than putting it down to a growth spurt. Seek urgent medical care if vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid or deep breathing, fruity-smelling breath, marked drowsiness or reduced responsiveness develops. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If your child''s bigger appetite comes with weight loss, unusual thirst or frequent urination: that combination isn''t a growth spurt. Contact your child''s healthcare professional promptly — ideally the same day — for advice and assessment. Seek urgent medical care if vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid or deep breathing, fruity-smelling breath, marked drowsiness or reduced responsiveness develops. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — your child''s appetite doesn''t need to change what you do for yourself this week. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you, even while offering your child extra helpings of the same foods. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be a bigger week than usual. Offer more of what''s already on the table, watch the pattern rather than any single meal, and take a genuine cluster of red-flag signs seriously rather than a bigger appetite on its own.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A toddler who suddenly wants seconds at every meal, or asks for a snack sooner than usual, isn''t doing anything unusual — appetite swings in both directions at this age, and a stretch of noticeably bigger hunger is simply the other side of the same variability that sometimes shows up as a smaller appetite instead. Whether it lines up with an actual stretch of faster growth or not, the response is the same either way, so there''s no need to try to confirm which one this is. The same choices still apply: the adult decides what food is offered, and broadly when and where it''s served. Your child decides whether to eat and how much — including asking for more.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked scrambled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked carrot", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked beetroot, mashed into curd or hummus made with previously introduced ingredients", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed banana", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a dal makhani, lentils cooked until very soft", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild chicken keema, minced and boneless, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "An extra small helping of whatever''s already on the table"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Offering more without overdoing it", "notes": "When the extra hunger is genuine, the simplest response is more of what''s already part of the rotation — another small helping of dal, rice, roti, fruit or curd — rather than reaching for something sweeter or more processed to fill the gap. Worth knowing: Serving a bigger portion of a familiar food doesn''t need any new preparation caution — keep it to the same textures and cuts you''d normally use for your child. For your child: It''s fine to simply refill the plate a second time without turning it into a bigger production — a toddler who''s genuinely hungry usually just wants more of the same, not something new.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with thin egg omelette strips on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sabut moong dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "lunch_b": "Rice with a well-cooked fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked yam (suran), mashed with curd or a little smooth hummus", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed papaya", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a lauki chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a boneless mutton keema, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What actually helps growth", "notes": "A plate built around a grain, a dal or other legume, and some milk or dairy already covers a lot of what growth actually draws on — there''s no need to chase an exact number to get this right. For your child: Iron-rich foods — dal, dark leafy greens, egg, chicken — are absorbed better when paired with a vitamin-C food in the same meal, like tomato, orange, amla, or a squeeze of lemon. A small pairing, but it goes a long way, especially on days built mostly around plant foods. Combining cereals with pulses or legumes — and including milk, curd, paneer, egg, fish or meat according to your family''s diet — helps provide a useful range of protein and other nutrients without requiring special products or precise calculations.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a tomato-based soft-cooked spinach", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken in a tomato-based spinach curry", "nourishment_break_a": "Very ripe guava, with the hard seed centre removed, cut into small soft pieces or mashed as needed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely grated beetroot", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a methi-moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Hunger doesn''t always follow the clock", "notes": "A toddler may ask for food at an expected mealtime, after active play, during a disrupted routine, or while seeking comfort. Timing alone can''t tell you whether the hunger is \"real.\" Respond calmly: if food was offered recently, you can first check whether your child needs connection, rest, water, or a change of activity. If they still seem hungry, it''s reasonable to offer a familiar snack or bring the next meal forward. For you: Eating for comfort sometimes is human and doesn''t need guilt or correction. You may choose food, rest, connection, or another form of care, according to what feels helpful in that moment.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina uttapam with vegetables", "breakfast_b": "Semolina uttapam with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a lobia curry, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with a mild chicken curry, boneless and finely shredded", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked pumpkin, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A soft cracker with a thin spread of hummus, only if its ingredients have already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild egg curry, egg fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Vegetable soup"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When it''s not a growth spurt", "notes": "A bigger appetite that comes with steady or increasing weight, energy, and general wellbeing is ordinary and needs nothing beyond feeding it. A bigger appetite alongside weight loss, unusual thirst, frequent urination, or a child who seems increasingly tired rather than more energetic is a different picture. Contact your child''s healthcare professional promptly — ideally the same day — for advice and assessment. Seek urgent medical care if vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid or deep breathing, fruity-smelling breath, marked drowsiness or reduced responsiveness develops. Worth knowing: Some steroid medicines can temporarily increase appetite. Continue the medicine exactly as prescribed, and don''t change the dose or stop it because of increased hunger. Ask the prescribing clinician or pharmacist if the appetite change is extreme, comes with unusual thirst or urination, or concerns you for any other reason.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with a mild sambar", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with fully cooked egg mixed into the filling", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower", "nourishment_break_a": "Ripe chikoo (sapota), with all seeds removed, cut into small soft pieces or mashed as needed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft-cooked, mashed apple", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a tinda sabzi and a small bowl of dal or curd", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a light chicken bhuna, boneless and finely shredded", "still_hungry": "Mashed avocado on soft toast"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Keeping it simple", "notes": "None of this needs to turn into a bigger week than usual. Keep offering the same balanced rotation of family food, let seconds happen without comment, and let the appetite settle on its own timeline, while watching the broader pattern of energy, wellbeing and growth. Growth itself is best tracked the way it always has been — through your child''s regular growth checks over time, not by trying to read a single hungrier week as a verdict on how they''re doing.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya khichdi", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable daliya khichdi with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chole curry, chickpeas cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a few finely ground almonds stirred in, only if almond has already been introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "Sweet lime (mosambi) segments, seeds removed, cut into soft pieces", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed vegetable curry and dal or paneer", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a boneless mutton curry, finely shredded, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Paneer bhurji"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did this week actually feel like a bigger-appetite stretch, or did it settle back to normal on its own — and either way, what made it easiest to just offer more without overthinking it?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Keeping up with a suddenly hungrier toddler takes real effort — more cooking, more portions, more trips back to the kitchen — worth acknowledging even though nothing about it looks unusual from the outside.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 86: Eating well when your toddler won't stay at the table
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   86,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler won''t stay at the table',
   '"A toddler meal does not need to be long to count. A short, calm period of seated eating may be enough on some days."',
   'Toddlers this age are built for movement, not stillness, and attention spans are short. There is no universal number of minutes a child must remain at the table for a meal to count — some meals may be brief. Look instead at whether your child gets regular opportunities to eat, eats comfortably, and is growing as expected. This is a different question from picky eating: it''s not about which foods are on the plate, but how long any meal, even a favourite, can hold their attention. You decide what food is offered, when it is offered and where eating happens — the schedule, the seat, and keeping screens and toys out of the way. A consistent, strapped-in seat facing the family does more work than reminders to sit still. Your child decides whether to eat and how much. If your child leaves the safe eating seat, calmly treat the meal as finished rather than following them with food — another eating opportunity will come at the next planned meal or snack. Chasing a wandering toddler with the spoon, bargaining with dessert, forcing a clean plate, or leaning on a screen to buy a few extra minutes all tend to backfire — they shift attention away from hunger and fullness and turn mealtime into a negotiation instead. Eating always stays a seated, closely supervised activity — never while walking or riding in a moving stroller or car. Use every restraint supplied with the child''s high chair or booster, and never rely on the tray alone. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If meals are consistently very stressful, your child repeatedly coughs or chokes while eating, has persistent or distressing gagging, regularly vomits, avoids particular textures, or isn''t growing as expected: that''s worth raising with your child''s doctor. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — a shorter or more mobile mealtime for your child doesn''t need to change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Set the schedule and the seat, let your child decide whether and how much to eat, calmly end the meal if they leave the seat, and keep an eye on the broader pattern of eating and growth rather than any single meal.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Toddlers have short attention spans, and some meals may be brief. There is no universal number of minutes a child must remain at the table. Look instead at whether your child receives regular opportunities to eat, eats comfortably, and is growing as expected. This is different from picky eating — it''s not about which foods are on the plate, it''s about how long any meal, even a favourite one, can hold their attention. You decide what food is offered, when it is offered and where eating happens. Your child decides whether to eat and how much. If your child leaves the safe eating seat, calmly treat the meal as finished rather than following them with food. Another eating opportunity will come at the next planned meal or snack.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with a small bowl of curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a fully cooked boiled egg, chopped or cut into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe plum, peeled and diced", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed sweet potato", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong-masoor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild egg bhurji", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal on the side"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Setting what''s actually yours to set", "notes": "You decide what food is offered, when it is offered and where eating happens — a regular time, a safe seat, no screens or toys competing for attention. Your child decides whether to eat and how much. If your child leaves the safe eating seat, calmly treat the meal as finished rather than following them with food; another eating opportunity will come at the next planned meal or snack. Worth knowing: Keep the chair strapped and away from tables, counters or walls your child could push off from to tip it, and never leave your child unattended in it, even for a moment. For your child: A consistent seat — a high chair or booster, strapped in, facing the rest of the family — helps more than reminders to sit still. The seat itself does some of the work.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dhokla with a fully cooked boiled egg, chopped or cut into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a black chana curry, chickpeas cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with a well-cooked, boneless chicken piece in a light curry", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked lauki, mashed into thick curd or dal", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely crumbled paneer mixed into mashed potato", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a toor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a boneless chicken keema, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "About the food that ends up on the floor", "notes": "Food throwing does not always mean your child was not hungry. It may reflect exploration, fullness, frustration, communication, or testing what happens next. Place only a small amount of food on the tray, calmly remind them that food stays on the tray, and remove the thrown item. If throwing continues and your child appears finished, end the meal without anger or negotiation. Worth knowing: A calm, consistent response works better than a big reaction in either direction — a dramatic \"no!\" or big laughter can both turn throwing into a game worth repeating. For your child: Offer milk as part of the child''s usual eating routine rather than allowing frequent sipping that replaces meals. Around two cups of cow''s milk a day is commonly advised after 12 months, although individual needs and breastfeeding patterns differ. If milk appears to be crowding out meals or iron-rich foods, discuss it with your child''s clinician.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with a tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with a fully cooked boiled egg, chopped or cut into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum", "nourishment_break_a": "Seedless watermelon, mashed or cut into thin, soft pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated cucumber", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana-palak curry", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a boneless fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Vegetable soup"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What tends to backfire", "notes": "Chasing a wandering toddler with the spoon, offering dessert as a reward for staying seated, or insisting the plate be finished before getting up all tend to backfire — they shift the goal from actual hunger to negotiation, and can make mealtimes feel like a contest rather than something to look forward to. A screen propped up to buy a few extra minutes at the table doesn''t reliably help either — it tends to distract from hunger and fullness cues rather than solve the sitting problem. For you: None of this needs a perfect response every time. A meal that ends earlier than planned, or a day with more mess than usual, isn''t a sign anything is going wrong.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bhindi", "nourishment_break_a": "Diced ripe papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A plain cracker that softens easily, without whole seeds or hard pieces, with finely crumbled paneer", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an urad dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild chicken curry, boneless and finely shredded", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Keeping it safe while it''s chaotic", "notes": "A toddler who''s up and wandering with food in hand, or eating from a moving stroller or car seat, isn''t just a mealtime-behaviour issue — it''s a real choking risk. Eating stays a seated, watched activity every time, even for a quick bite. Worth knowing: Use every restraint supplied by the manufacturer, including the crotch strap when present, and never rely on the tray as a restraint. Keep the chair away from tables, counters, walls or other surfaces the child could push against or climb onto. For your child: An adult should remain close and attentive throughout the meal — never leave your child alone in a high chair, even for a moment.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats idli", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats idli with a fully cooked boiled egg, chopped or cut into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked carrot served with thick curd or smooth hummus, hummus only if sesame has already been introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed pear", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a spiced egg bhurji, egg fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft roti, only if its ingredients have already been introduced safely"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s more than a phase", "notes": "A toddler who eats reasonably well across the day, receives regular opportunities to eat, and is growing as expected may be well within the ordinary range, even when seated meals are brief — this is a phase to design around, not a problem to fix. Seek professional advice if meals are consistently very stressful, your child repeatedly coughs or chokes while eating, experiences persistent or distressing gagging, regularly vomits, avoids particular textures, or is not growing as expected. This is about how long and how the meal goes, not about being a picky eater with specific foods — a related but different topic, and its own kind of ordinary too.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a squeeze of lemon", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked french beans", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked french beans", "nourishment_break_a": "Ripe, peeled kiwi cut into small, soft pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely crumbled paneer with a soft roti strip", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Paneer bhurji"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. How long did meals actually last this week, and did letting go of the \"stay until everyone''s done\" expectation change how mealtimes felt?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Resetting your own expectations about how long a meal "should" take is its own kind of work — worth noticing, even when the mealtime itself looked unremarkable from the outside.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 87: Moving from bottle and sippy cup to an open cup
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   87,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Moving from bottle and sippy cup to an open cup',
   '"There''s no single day this needs to happen — a steady, patient shift toward an open cup protects your child''s teeth and appetite, whether it takes a week or several months."',
   'Pediatric guidance encourages introducing a cup when solids begin and gradually reducing bottle feeds, with the overall transition commonly completed between 12 and 18 months, and an open cup generally encouraged by around age two. Dental guidance particularly discourages on-demand or nighttime bottle use beyond 12 months because prolonged exposure can increase the risk of tooth decay. The transition is usually gradual, and taking longer does not mean a parent has failed — but it''s worth continuing steadily. Finish the last milk feed before your child lies down, then brush the teeth with a small smear of fluoride toothpaste. After brushing, offer only plain water if your child is thirsty. Do not put a child to sleep with milk, formula, juice or another sweetened drink in a bottle or cup. For many toddlers, around 16-24 oz of cow''s milk a day is a commonly used range, although individual needs and breastfeeding patterns differ. Offering milk at planned meals or snacks rather than allowing continuous sipping can help prevent it from crowding out iron-rich foods. If your child drinks substantially more milk, eats very few iron-rich foods, appears unusually pale or tired, or has previously had low iron, discuss this with the child''s clinician. A valved sippy cup requires continued sucking, so it''s best treated as a temporary learning tool rather than a permanent drinking method. An open cup or suitable straw cup gives your child opportunities to practise different drinking skills, rather than guaranteeing any particular outcome. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'Arrange a dental assessment if you notice chalky white areas, brown or dark spots, pits, tooth pain, swelling or sensitivity. A bottle habit that feels impossible to change is also worth discussing with your child''s dentist or pediatrician. Children should receive regular preventive dental care even when no visible problem is present. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — working on your child''s cup transition doesn''t need to change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Offer milk in a cup, brush after the last feed before bed, and keep working through the transition steadily — a slower pace is fine, but the direction should stay forward.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Many toddlers complete the bottle-to-cup transition between 12 and 18 months, although the pace varies. Nighttime and continuously available bottles deserve particular attention because they increase dental and feeding concerns. If your child is still using a bottle beyond this window, there''s no need for shame or panic — but it''s a good time to continue the transition consistently and seek guidance if it feels difficult. If your child is already off the bottle, this week''s cup and dental guidance still applies — the ideas here work just as well for refining an existing cup routine. The longer a bottle habit continues, the more attached a child may become to it. This is one practical reason to continue the transition steadily, alongside protecting the teeth and preventing bottles from displacing meals.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "Grated beetroot, cooked soft and mixed into rice", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with ripe guava pulp, thoroughly deseeded and mashed", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a matar-paneer curry, peas cooked soft and lightly mashed, paneer crumbled", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild lamb curry, boneless and finely shredded, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why bedtime bottles matter most", "notes": "A bottle or sippy cup carried through the day, or sipped slowly at bedtime, keeps milk in contact with new teeth for long stretches — one of the clearest risk factors for early tooth decay. This is true for a bedtime bottle of plain milk too, not just sugary drinks. Worth knowing: Finish the last milk feed before your child lies down, then brush the teeth with a small smear of fluoride toothpaste. After brushing, offer only plain water if your child is thirsty. For your child: Do not put your child to sleep with milk, formula, juice or another sweetened drink in a bottle or cup — a bedtime bottle, if one continues, should contain only water.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with grated coconut", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a boiled egg, chopped into small pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked yam (suran)", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked yam (suran)", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked pumpkin mixed into a small bowl of dal", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed chikoo, all seeds removed", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and grated bottle gourd", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a boneless lamb keema, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft roti, only if its ingredients have already been introduced safely"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "About the sippy cup specifically", "notes": "A spill-proof cup containing milk, juice or another carbohydrate-containing drink can create repeated exposure for the teeth when it''s sipped throughout the day. Between meals, plain water is the safer choice. Once your child can manage it, an open cup or a straw cup is generally the better next step, rather than treating the sippy cup as a permanent stop. A valved sippy cup requires continued sucking, so it is best treated as a temporary learning tool rather than a permanent drinking method. An open cup or suitable straw cup allows your child to practise different drinking skills over time. For your child: A short, low, wide cup with a small amount of liquid is easiest to start with — expect spills, and let your child practice at meals where mess is already expected.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya khichdi with grated carrot", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable daliya khichdi with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked tinda", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked tinda", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe custard apple (sitaphal), deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with peeled, very finely grated raw apple", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked lauki", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild egg curry, egg fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "How much milk, and how it''s offered", "notes": "For many toddlers, around 16-24 oz of cow''s milk a day is a commonly used range, although individual needs and breastfeeding patterns differ. Offering milk at planned meals or snacks rather than allowing continuous sipping can help prevent it from crowding out iron-rich foods — milk is calorie-dense but low in iron. For you: This isn''t about restricting milk itself — it''s about how and when it''s offered. If your child drinks substantially more milk, eats very few iron-rich foods, appears unusually pale or tired, or has previously had low iron, discuss this with your child''s clinician.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with a boiled egg, chopped into small pieces, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked sweet potato, mashed with a spoon of dal", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft sweet-lime (mosambi) segments, seeds and tough membranes completely removed", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and grated lauki", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild chicken keema, minced and boneless, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Vegetable soup"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Keeping cup practice safe", "notes": "Among emergency-department injuries associated with bottles, pacifiers and sippy cups, falls while using the product were the most common mechanism. A toddler can seriously injure the mouth by falling with a bottle, spout or straw in it, so drinking should remain a seated activity. Worth knowing: Keep drinking, like eating, a seated activity — not while walking, climbing, or moving through the house. A calm \"let''s sit down for that\" works better than taking the cup away entirely. For your child: Stay nearby during cup practice, offer only a small amount at first, and keep your child seated. Spills are expected and do not need a big reaction.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina uttapam with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Semolina uttapam with a boiled egg, chopped into small pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked bottle gourd", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bottle gourd", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe papaya, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe pear, skin removed", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a spiced egg curry, egg fully cooked", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal on the side"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "There''s no single deadline", "notes": "If your child hasn''t yet made the full switch, that''s not a sign anything has gone wrong — pediatric sources are explicit that this is meant to be gradual and individualized. If an attempt becomes highly stressful, pause briefly, return to the previous manageable step and try again consistently. If the transition remains extremely difficult, ask your child''s pediatrician or dentist for support. Dropping one bottle or sippy feeding at a time, starting with the one your child cares about least, tends to work better than an abrupt full stop. A comforting bedtime routine — extra cuddles, a song, a story — can take over the soothing role a bottle used to play, without needing the bottle itself.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya khichdi with grated beetroot", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable daliya khichdi with a fully cooked egg bhurji stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked spinach", "nourishment_break_a": "Ripe kiwi, peeled and cut into small, soft pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely crumbled paneer stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a rajma dal, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a boneless chicken bhuna, fully cooked, finely shredded", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Where is your child''s cup routine right now, and is there one bottle or sippy feeding this week that might be a reasonable next one to gently phase out?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Letting go of the bottle can feel bigger than it looks from the outside — it''s often tied to memories of the earliest, most intense months. Noticing that, without needing to rush past it, is its own kind of care.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 88: Rethinking salt and sugar in your toddler's everyday food
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   88,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Rethinking salt and sugar in your toddler''s everyday food',
   '"Your toddler''s food doesn''t need extra salt or sugar to taste good to them — their palate is still being set right now, and less of both supports a steadier appetite for real food later."',
   'Guidance recommends avoiding added sugar before age two. Added sugars include sugar, jaggery, honey and syrups added during cooking, manufacturing or serving. WHO''s broader term "free sugars" also includes the sugars in fruit juice and fruit-juice concentrates. The naturally occurring sugars in intact whole fruit and plain milk are treated differently, although fruit juice should still be limited. For children two and older, guidance points to under about 25g (6 teaspoons) of added sugar a day. Toddlers need considerably less salt than adults. Some national guidance uses a maximum of about 2g of salt a day for children aged one to three, including salt already present in food — well below a typical adult limit. Because recommendations and food labels may use either "salt" or "sodium," the most practical approach is to keep added salt modest and limit high-sodium packaged foods, stock cubes, sauces, pickles, papad and processed meats, rather than trying to calculate an exact daily total. Toddlers require much less food than adults, so adult levels of sodium can be reached quickly within their smaller overall intake. Keeping salty foods limited also helps the child become familiar with the natural flavours of ordinary food. How often sugar reaches the teeth matters as much as how much — frequent sipping or snacking on sweet foods and drinks through the day gives cavity-causing bacteria more chances to produce the acid that wears down enamel, which is one more reason grazing on sweet snacks is more of a dental concern than the same amount of sugar eaten in one sitting. Packaged snacks, restaurant food, sauces and sweetened drinks are common sources of added salt and sugar, but home-cooked food can contribute too — a heavily salted curry, dal, pulao or chutney adds up just as easily. Keep the family meal modestly salted, avoid adding extra salt to your toddler''s serving, and let adults adjust their own portions separately if needed. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother.',
   'If your child eats packaged snacks often, or seems to strongly prefer very salty or very sweet foods: that''s a reasonable starting point for this week''s changes, not something that needs fixing all at once. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — reducing your child''s added sugar doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you — the same everyday habits (checking labels, keeping the salt shaker off the table) tend to help both you and your child. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. The goal before age two is to avoid routinely offering foods with added sugar — if your child is given a small sweet at a celebration, there''s no need for guilt or compensation, simply return to the usual routine afterward. Keep the salt shaker off the table, keep the family meal modestly salted, and avoid adding extra salt to your toddler''s serving.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Added sugar is best avoided entirely before age two. Added sugars include sugar, jaggery, honey and syrups added during cooking, manufacturing or serving — WHO''s broader term \"free sugars\" also includes the sugars in fruit juice and fruit-juice concentrates, so fruit juice is still worth limiting. The naturally occurring sugars in intact whole fruit and plain milk are treated differently, and don''t need limiting the same way. After age two, guidance points to roughly 25g (about 6 teaspoons) of added sugar a day. A ripe banana, a bowl of curd, or a glass of plain milk isn''t \"sugar\" in the sense this week is about — the target is what gets added on top of ordinary food, not fruit or dairy in their natural form. This doesn''t call for a completely separate meal for your toddler — but home-cooked food can still be a meaningful source of salt, so keep the family meal modestly salted and avoid adding extra salt to your toddler''s serving.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with a small bowl of dal", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Vegetable pulao with a small bowl of curd", "lunch_b": "Vegetable pulao with boneless, finely shredded chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe banana, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with ripe mango cut into small, soft pieces", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a toor dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a boneless chicken curry, finely shredded, mildly spiced", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why toddlers need so much less salt", "notes": "Toddlers need considerably less salt than adults. Some national guidance uses a maximum of about 2g of salt a day for children aged one to three, including salt already present in food. Toddlers require much less food than adults, so adult levels of sodium can be reached quickly within their smaller overall intake. Keeping salty foods limited also helps your child become familiar with the natural flavours of ordinary food, and early salt exposure appears to shape how salty food needs to taste to satisfy a child later on. For your child: It doesn''t have to taste salty to be high in sodium — some packaged snacks and processed foods carry a surprising amount of sodium without an obviously salty taste, so \"it doesn''t taste salty\" isn''t a reliable guide on its own. Chutneys, pickles and similar condiments are often saltier than they taste — make a low-salt version for your toddler''s portion, or offer just a small amount.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with a mild, low-salt coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Idli with a boiled egg, chopped into small pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked beetroot", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked beetroot, mashed with a spoon of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe plum", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong-masoor dal", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild mutton curry, boneless and finely shredded, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Vegetable soup"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Why frequency matters as much as amount", "notes": "How often sugar reaches the teeth matters as much as how much — frequent sipping or snacking on sweet foods and drinks through the day gives cavity-causing bacteria more chances to produce the acid that wears down enamel. If a sweet food is offered, serving it with a planned meal is generally gentler on the teeth than allowing repeated grazing or sipping throughout the day. This is the same idea behind last week''s guidance on bottles and sippy cups — steady exposure across the day matters more than any single sweet food or drink.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with a squeeze of lemon", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked carrot", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked apple, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely ground walnut stirred in, only if walnut has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma curry, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a fully cooked chicken curry, boneless and finely shredded", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal on the side"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Where it''s actually hiding", "notes": "Packaged snacks, sweetened drinks and restaurant food are common sources of added sugar and sodium, and a heavily salted or sweetened home-cooked dish adds up too. Checking the \"added sugars\" line on a label (a separate line from total sugars, which includes natural sugar from fruit or dairy) and keeping the family meal modestly seasoned are two of the most useful everyday habits. For you: A \"sugar-free\" or \"no added sugar\" claim doesn''t automatically make a product suitable for a toddler. Check the ingredient list for jaggery, honey, syrups, fruit-juice concentrate and low- or no-calorie sweeteners — best avoided for children under two unless specifically advised by their clinician. Also check the sodium content and the overall ingredient list rather than relying only on the claim on the front.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla with a low-salt tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dhokla with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked lauki", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked lauki", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked pumpkin, mashed with a spoon of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a spiced egg bhurji, egg fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Sweets from relatives and festivals", "notes": "One small sweet at a celebration does not create the same overall pattern as frequent sugary snacks or drinks. Frequency and repeated exposure matter, especially for dental health. If a relative offers your child something sweet, respond calmly rather than treating it as a crisis. The goal before age two is still to avoid routinely offering added sugar — an occasional celebration exception doesn''t undo that, and doesn''t call for guilt or compensation afterward. For you: Toddlers do not need sweets in order to feel included. At celebrations, participation can come through sitting with the family, sharing an age-appropriate regular food, singing, playing or enjoying the ritual itself.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with ripe pear cut into small, soft pieces", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked egg bhurji stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked brinjal", "nourishment_break_a": "Yam (suran), fully cooked until soft, mashed with a spoon of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with ripe kiwi, peeled and cut into small, soft pieces", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor-palak curry", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a well-cooked fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft roti, only if its ingredients have already been introduced safely"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "What this doesn''t mean", "notes": "This doesn''t require cooking a completely separate meal. Prepare the family food with modest salt, set aside your toddler''s portion before adding extra salt, salty sauces or very spicy seasoning, and let adults adjust their own servings afterward. Added sugar is still best avoided for your child before age two. Keep the salt shaker off the table, keep sweets occasional rather than daily once your child is past two, and let your family''s modestly seasoned food do most of the work — none of this needs to be complicated.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with a low-salt mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked french beans", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe mango, mashed if needed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated beetroot", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a toor dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild egg curry, egg fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Where does most of the added sugar or salt in your child''s week actually come from — home cooking, packaged snacks, or something else?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Noticing where sugar and salt quietly show up isn''t about guilt — most of it is designed to be easy to miss. Paying attention once is often enough to start shifting the pattern.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 89: Making sure your toddler gets enough vitamin D
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   89,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Making sure your toddler gets enough vitamin D',
   '"Vitamin D supports growing bones. Food, outdoor exposure and — when recommended — a correctly dosed supplement can each contribute to meeting your child''s needs."',
   'Vitamin D''s best-established job is helping the body absorb and use calcium and phosphorus for bone growth — the main reason pediatric guidance treats it as important through the toddler years, not just infancy. Recommendations differ by authority: some international references use 600 IU (15 micrograms) a day for children over one year, while the revised Indian Academy of Pediatrics guidance discusses an estimated requirement of around 400 IU a day, ideally met through food and sunlight, and other Indian expert guidance supports routine supplementation for young children. Because the recommendations aren''t completely uniform, ask your child''s clinician what applies locally and to your child''s circumstances. Some sources also link vitamin D to immune function, but pediatric bodies are explicit that this evidence isn''t strong enough yet to base supplement decisions on — bone health remains the well-established reason. India gets plenty of sunlight, but vitamin D deficiency is still common, especially in cities — a real, well-documented pattern. Darker skin needs meaningfully more sun exposure to make the same amount of vitamin D as lighter skin, and time spent indoors, air pollution, and covering clothing all reduce how much UVB actually reaches the skin, even on a sunny day. Sunlight can contribute to vitamin D production, but there is no brief weekly exposure that reliably works for every child. Indian pediatric guidance discusses regular midday exposure on multiple days each week — not only a short amount across an entire week — but the amount needed varies with skin tone, clothing, season, location, pollution and the area of skin exposed. Encourage regular outdoor activity, avoid sunburn, and follow your child''s clinician''s advice rather than using a fixed exposure time as a prescription. Diet alone rarely covers a toddler''s vitamin D needs — even good sources like egg yolk and fatty fish contribute modestly, and most milk in India isn''t fortified with vitamin D unless a specifically fortified brand is used. Guidelines differ on routine supplementation after infancy: some favour meeting the requirement through food and sunlight where feasible, while others recommend a daily preventive supplement for young children. Your child''s diet, outdoor exposure, health conditions and locally available fortified foods should guide the decision with their clinician. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. This week''s meals include foods associated with vitamin D, but their vitamin D content varies and this menu isn''t intended to guarantee a particular daily dose.',
   'If your child has limited sun exposure, follows a vegetarian diet without eggs or fish, or you''re simply unsure whether a supplement is needed: that''s worth discussing with your child''s doctor rather than starting or dosing a supplement on your own. Routine vitamin D blood testing isn''t usually needed for every healthy toddler. A clinician may recommend testing when there are symptoms, significant risk factors, poor growth, bone concerns, chronic liver or kidney disease, malabsorption, or medicines that affect vitamin D metabolism. Seek medical advice if your child has persistent bone pain, delayed motor progress, bowed legs, unusual fractures, muscle weakness or poor growth. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s vitamin D doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Regular outdoor time, a handful of vitamin-D-friendly foods across the week, and a conversation with your child''s doctor about sun exposure and whether a supplement fits — that covers it.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. Fatty fish and egg yolk are useful vitamin D sources when they''re part of your family''s diet, but they''re not required; cook them all the way through. The fish choices are alternatives across the week, not a recommendation to serve fish every day. Aim for approximately two to three servings of varied lower-mercury fish during the week, following local advisories, and use the egg or chicken alternatives on the remaining days.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin D''s best-established job is helping the body absorb and use calcium and phosphorus for bone growth — the main reason pediatric guidance treats it as important through the toddler years, not just infancy. Recommendations differ by authority: some international references use 600 IU a day for children over one year, while revised Indian guidance discusses a lower estimated requirement met mainly through food and sunlight — ask your child''s clinician what applies locally. Some sources also link vitamin D to immune function, but pediatric bodies are explicit that this evidence isn''t strong enough yet to base supplement decisions on — bone health remains the well-established reason.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and a soft-cooked mushroom curry", "nourishment_break_a": "Thick curd with mashed ripe papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped or mashed into a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild Indian or Atlantic mackerel curry — not king mackerel — fully cooked, carefully deboned and finely flaked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "How much sun actually helps", "notes": "Sunlight can contribute to vitamin D production, but there''s no brief weekly exposure that reliably works for every child. Indian pediatric guidance discusses regular midday exposure on multiple days each week, but the amount needed varies with skin tone, clothing, season, location, pollution and the area of skin exposed — a fixed exposure time isn''t a prescription, so follow your child''s clinician''s advice for your family''s situation. For your child: Outdoor play has benefits beyond vitamin D. Choose conditions appropriate for your local climate, avoid sunburn and excessive heat, and use shade, clothing or sunscreen according to your child''s sun-safety needs.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats idli with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum", "nourishment_break_a": "Grated paneer mixed into mashed sweet potato", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft-cooked mushroom mixed into a small bowl of dal", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild chicken keema, boneless and fully cooked", "still_hungry": "Vegetable soup"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Plenty of sun, still common deficiency", "notes": "India gets plenty of sunlight, but vitamin D deficiency is still common, especially in cities — a real, well-documented pattern. Darker skin needs meaningfully more sun exposure to make the same amount of vitamin D as lighter skin, and time spent indoors, air pollution, and covering clothing all reduce how much actually reaches the skin, even on a sunny day.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina uttapam with coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Semolina uttapam with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked cauliflower", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower", "nourishment_break_a": "Ripe pear, peeled and cut into small, soft pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked tinda", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild salmon curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal on the side"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What food alone can and can''t do", "notes": "Diet alone rarely covers a toddler''s vitamin D needs. Even foods described as good sources — egg yolk and fatty fish — contribute modestly, and most milk in India isn''t fortified with vitamin D unless a specifically fortified brand is used. UV-exposed mushrooms can provide vitamin D when the product is specifically labelled as such; ordinary mushrooms shouldn''t be assumed to provide a meaningful amount. For you: A vegetarian diet without eggs or fish is linked with a higher chance of low vitamin D — this isn''t a reason to change your family''s diet, just useful context for the conversation with your child''s doctor about food sources and whether a supplement makes sense.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with a fully cooked egg bhurji mixed into the filling", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked lauki", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked lauki", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed potato with finely grated paneer", "nourishment_break_b": "A fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped or mashed into mashed potato", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a simple egg curry, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Whether a supplement makes sense", "notes": "Guidelines differ on routine supplementation after infancy. Some favour meeting the requirement through food and sunlight where feasible; others recommend a daily preventive supplement for young children. Your child''s diet, outdoor exposure, health conditions and locally available fortified foods should guide the decision with their clinician. Worth knowing: If a supplement is started, follow the prescribed dose exactly — there''s a real ceiling above which vitamin D can cause harm, and higher-dose products exist for diagnosed deficiency under medical supervision that shouldn''t be used for everyday prevention without guidance.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya khichdi with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable daliya khichdi with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked brinjal", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bhindi", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked mushroom, mashed with a spoon of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with ripe papaya cut into small, soft pieces or mashed", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild sardine curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft roti, only if its ingredients have already been introduced safely"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A common, manageable gap", "notes": "Low vitamin D levels have been reported in children across different Indian regions and income groups, although prevalence varies depending on the population studied and the laboratory threshold used — this isn''t a sign of anything done wrong, and it''s usually straightforward to address once you know it''s worth checking. A quick conversation at your child''s next checkup is often all it takes. Regular outdoor time on multiple days of the week, a handful of vitamin-D-friendly foods worked into the week, and a conversation with your child''s clinician about sun exposure and whether a supplement fits your family''s situation — that''s the whole approach, and it doesn''t call for a fixed exposure prescription or measuring anything precisely.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Idli with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked beetroot", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked french beans", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and mashed banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped or mashed into a small bowl of dal", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and soft-cooked lauki", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a boneless chicken curry, fully cooked and finely shredded", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. How much regular outdoor time does your child typically get in a week, and is vitamin D supplementation something worth discussing with your child''s clinician?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'A nutrient you can''t see or taste is easy to overlook — noticing it once, and asking a simple question at the next checkup, is a small thing that''s easy to let slip otherwise.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 90: Introducing your toddler to spices
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   90,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Introducing your toddler to spices',
   '"Aromatic spices like turmeric, cumin, coriander and cardamom can be part of your toddler''s food from early on — it''s chilli heat, not flavour itself, that needs to stay minimal and build up slowly."',
   'Aromatic spices — turmeric, cumin, coriander, cardamom, cinnamon, ginger, garlic and a mild garam masala — are widely considered fine to include in a toddler''s food from early on, in small amounts. No major pediatric body publishes a specific age rule for spice, and Indian complementary-feeding guidance is built around home-made, locally available food, moving toward the shared family diet by around a year — so a mild version of your usual cooking is very much in line with that, not something that calls for special caution. The general caution seen in pediatric nutrition guidance is against heavily seasoned, over-salted or over-sweetened food, not against spice itself. Chilli heat is the exception, and it works differently from the flavour of other spices. The compound that makes chilli hot activates pain receptors rather than taste receptors, so what a child feels from a hot chilli is closer to a burning sensation than a flavour — pediatric clinicians note that children generally tolerate this less well than adults do. This is why most guidance treats chilli, and large amounts of black pepper, separately from aromatic spices: hold back the heat for a toddler''s portion, and add it to the adult portion afterwards rather than leaving it out of the seasoning altogether. A few practical points are worth knowing alongside this. Whole cardamom pods, cloves and peppercorns are firm enough to be a choking risk if left whole in a dish, so remove them from your toddler''s portion or use the ground form; the same caution reasonably extends to other whole, woody spices. Nutmeg is fine in the small pinch used in ordinary cooking but should never be given in large amounts or used as a home remedy. Raw garlic and ginger have an intense flavour and may cause discomfort for some children — cooked forms are usually easier to introduce, but tolerance varies. Packaged spice blends need a closer look than a single spice does. Garam masala and curry powders vary widely from one brand to the next, so choose a finely ground blend without added salt and with little or no chilli for your child''s portion, and avoid a blend containing anything your child has reacted to before — whole fragments can occasionally remain in a coarsely ground blend, so it''s worth checking before use. When offering an unfamiliar spice, begin with a small culinary amount so your child can become familiar with the flavour. Ordinary spices don''t usually need to be introduced one at a time or separated by several days, the way priority allergens do. If using a spice blend, read the full ingredient list first: when its ingredients are familiar and already tolerated, use a small amount, and if it contains an unfamiliar ingredient, consider introducing that ingredient separately first. Seek clinical guidance if your child has a diagnosed food allergy or has reacted to a spice or blend ingredient before. If loose stools, vomiting or stomach discomfort appears after a new food, pause that food and observe your child rather than assuming the spice is responsible. Seek medical advice if symptoms are persistent, severe, keep returning with the same ingredient, include blood, or come with poor drinking, reduced urination or unusual sleepiness. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. This week''s meals use a familiar Indian spice palette with the heat kept mild for the toddler''s portion; adjust the amount of chilli and other seasoning in the adult portion to your family''s usual taste.',
   'If your child has a diagnosed digestive condition, reflux, or a known sensitivity to a particular spice: introduce new spices even more gradually and check with your child''s doctor before adding anything that has caused a problem before. Stop the food if your child develops hives, facial or lip swelling, or repeated vomiting soon after eating, and seek urgent medical advice — don''t give the food again until your child has been assessed. Breathing difficulty, wheezing, tongue or throat swelling, collapse or unusual unresponsiveness is an emergency: call local emergency services immediately. Most culinary spices aren''t priority allergens, but a genuine allergic reaction to a spice or a blend ingredient is still possible, so these signs are worth watching for regardless. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s food doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Keep the aromatic spices in, hold the chilli back a little longer, and let your child''s own reaction guide the pace.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. Cook them all the way through, keep chicken boneless and finely shredded, and keep fish deboned and finely flaked; the two fish choices this week are alternatives, not a requirement, so use the chicken or egg option instead if you''d rather.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Aromatic spices such as turmeric, cumin, coriander, cinnamon, ginger and garlic can be introduced in small culinary amounts from the complementary-feeding period. Children do not need bland food indefinitely. Keep the toddler''s serving mild, low in salt and free from whole spice pieces, then build stronger flavours according to your child''s comfort. Worth knowing: Choose finely ground spice blends without added salt and with little or no chilli. Always check the ingredient list before adding one to your child''s food.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a pinch of turmeric and cumin", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a mild dal tadka and soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken in a mild tomato gravy", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated carrot and a pinch of roasted cumin", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with finely grated paneer stirred in", "dinner_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild pomfret curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The heat, not the flavour", "notes": "Chilli heat works differently from the flavour of other spices. The compound that makes chilli hot activates pain receptors rather than taste receptors, so what a child feels from a hot chilli is closer to a burning sensation than a flavour — and pediatric clinicians note that children generally tolerate this less well than adults do. This is a large part of why most guidance treats chilli, and large amounts of black pepper, differently from aromatic spices: keep a toddler''s portion mild, and add real heat to the adult portion afterwards rather than leaving it out of the seasoning altogether. For your child: If the food is unexpectedly hot, stop offering it and provide a familiar bland food. Plain curd or milk, if tolerated, may soothe the burning sensation better than water; rice or soft roti may also help. Don''t pressure your child to keep eating the dish.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked sweet potato, mashed with a spoon of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana and a pinch of finely ground cardamom", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and soft-cooked carrot", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a lightly spiced egg curry, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal on the side"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A few things worth knowing", "notes": "A handful of practical points are worth keeping in mind rather than avoiding spice altogether. Whole cardamom pods, cloves and peppercorns are firm enough to be a choking risk if left whole in a dish — remove them from your toddler''s portion, or use the ground form instead; the same caution reasonably extends to other whole, woody spices such as cinnamon sticks or bay leaves. Nutmeg is fine in the small pinch used in ordinary cooking, but should never be given in large amounts or used as a home remedy — well beyond typical culinary use, it can cause real toxicity. Raw garlic and ginger have an intense flavour and may cause discomfort for some children — cooked forms are usually easier to introduce, but tolerance varies, so cook them through rather than adding them raw. Worth knowing: Spice used as a remedy is a different matter from spice used in cooking — concentrated preparations such as cumin water or a strong turmeric drink given for a specific ailment aren''t the same as a pinch of ground cumin or turmeric stirred into a meal. Check with your child''s doctor before using any spice as a remedy rather than as food.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with a mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and green peas cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe chikoo, all seeds removed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana curry, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild mackerel curry (Indian or Atlantic, not king mackerel), deboned and finely flaked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Trying something new", "notes": "When offering an unfamiliar spice, begin with a small culinary amount so your child can become familiar with the flavour — ordinary spices don''t usually need to be introduced one at a time or separated by several days, the way priority allergens do. If using a spice blend, read the full ingredient list first: when its ingredients are familiar and already tolerated, use a small amount, and if it contains an unfamiliar ingredient, consider introducing that ingredient separately first. Seek clinical guidance if your child has a diagnosed food allergy or has reacted to a spice or blend ingredient before. If loose stools, vomiting or stomach discomfort appears after a new food, pause that food and observe your child rather than assuming the spice is responsible — seek medical advice if symptoms are persistent, severe, keep returning with the same ingredient, include blood, or come with poor drinking, reduced urination or unusual sleepiness. Stop the food and seek urgent medical advice if your child develops hives, facial or lip swelling, or repeated vomiting soon after eating; breathing difficulty, wheezing, tongue or throat swelling, collapse or unusual unresponsiveness is an emergency — call local emergency services immediately, and don''t give the food again until your child has been assessed. Most culinary spices aren''t priority allergens, but a genuine allergic reaction to a spice or a blend ingredient is still possible, so these signs are worth watching for regardless.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia khichdi with a mild tempering", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia khichdi with a fully cooked egg bhurji stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked lauki", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked carrot, mashed with a spoon of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a boneless chicken curry, finely shredded and mildly spiced", "still_hungry": "Vegetable soup"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "One pot for the family", "notes": "You don''t need to cook two separate meals. Cook the aromatic base with modest salt and mild ground spices — turmeric, cumin, coriander, ginger-garlic — then set aside your toddler''s portion before adding chilli, a hot tempering, extra salt or strongly salted sauces for the adults. After setting the toddler''s portion aside, adjust its texture or consistency if needed, but don''t add extra salt, whole spices or chilli heat to it. For your child: Keep an eye on how your child takes to a milder version of the same meal everyone else is eating — most toddlers this age can manage the family''s food quite well once the heat is set aside.", "breakfast_a": "Oats uttapam with a mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Oats uttapam with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked snake gourd", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked snake gourd", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe chikoo, mashed, all seeds removed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated carrot and a pinch of cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a toor dal and soft-cooked tinda", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken in a lightly spiced gravy", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A gentle habit, not a rule", "notes": "There''s no need to keep your toddler''s food bland indefinitely, and no need to rush stronger flavours either. Most of this week''s guidance comes down to one simple habit: keep the aromatic spices in, hold the chilli back, and let your child''s own tolerance guide how quickly that changes. A pinch of turmeric or cumin in the family pot, chilli added at the end for the adults, and a little patience with new flavours — that''s really the whole approach, and it fits naturally into cooking you''re already doing.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal dhokla with a mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal dhokla with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked carrot", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked yellow pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with ripe guava pulp, thoroughly deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked pumpkin", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a soft scrambled egg mixed in, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Which spices does your toddler already enjoy in family meals, and is there one you''ve been holding back that might be worth trying in a small amount?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Spice is often treated as an all-or-nothing decision, when in practice it''s really a matter of degree — keeping the flavour in, and setting the heat aside a little longer.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 91: Understanding fat in your toddler's diet
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   91,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Understanding fat in your toddler''s diet',
   '"Toddlers need fat for energy, brain development and to absorb key vitamins — this is one age where cutting fat isn''t the healthy choice."',
   'Fat is an essential part of a toddler''s diet, not something to cut back on. It''s a concentrated source of energy for a stomach that''s still small, it''s needed to absorb vitamins A, D, E and K from food, and it supports the rapid brain development still under way at this age. Pediatric guidance is direct about this: a low-fat diet isn''t the healthy choice for a toddler the way it might be marketed to adults, and cutting fat too far can mean a child doesn''t get enough calories overall, not just less fat. Experts generally place a toddler''s fat intake somewhere in the low-to-mid 30s percent of daily calories, though the exact figure varies a little by source. If your child drinks cow''s milk, pasteurized, unsweetened whole milk is commonly recommended from 12 to 24 months — the fat is considered part of the point here, not a drawback. Breastfeeding can continue for as long as parent and child wish, and cow''s milk isn''t required when nutritional needs are already being met through breastfeeding and a varied diet; milk, curd and cheese all count toward dairy intake, so more milk isn''t automatically better, and a lot of milk can crowd out iron-rich food. A dairy alternative should be unsweetened and fortified with calcium and vitamin D, since not all plant drinks are nutritionally equivalent to cow''s milk. There''s a specific exception worth knowing: if your child has had excessive weight gain, or there''s a family history of obesity, high cholesterol or heart disease, it''s worth asking your child''s doctor whether an earlier switch to a lower-fat milk makes sense. Some newer research actually questions whether switching early helps in the way it was once assumed to, so treat this as a conversation for your child''s doctor rather than a decision to make on general low-fat instinct alone. Not all fats need equal attention. Unsaturated fats — found in cooking oils like groundnut, sesame or sunflower, in oily fish, and in nuts once they''re safely introduced — are the preferred everyday sources for a toddler. Saturated fat, found in ghee, butter and full-fat dairy, doesn''t need to be avoided, but is usually kept to a moderate share of the day''s food rather than the dominant one. Trans fat is the one type worth actively avoiding at any age — mainly vanaspati and products listing partially hydrogenated oils. Packaged and repeatedly deep-fried foods are also best limited, but their fat composition varies, so check the ingredient list rather than assuming every packaged food contains trans fat. Omega-3 fats — particularly DHA, found in oily fish — are closely linked with brain and eye development, and this window of rapid brain growth continues through the toddler years; walnuts, flaxseed and mustard oil provide a different omega-3 called ALA, which the body converts to DHA only in limited amounts, so these remain nutritious but aren''t a direct substitute for fish. Toddlers should not be placed on a deliberately low-fat diet, but that does not mean adding unlimited ghee, butter or oil. Include a variety of fats in age-appropriate family portions, with unsaturated oils, nut and seed pastes, avocado and lower-mercury oily fish providing useful everyday options. Ghee, butter and full-fat dairy can remain part of the diet, but they do not need to be added to every meal. Restricting fat too aggressively can reduce the energy density of a toddler''s diet; some commercially marketed low-fat foods may also contain added sugar, so it''s worth assessing the whole product rather than assuming "low-fat" automatically means healthier. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. This week''s meals include varied fats in age-appropriate family portions; additional enrichment is not required.',
   'If your child has had excessive weight gain, or there''s a family history of obesity, high cholesterol or heart disease: ask your child''s doctor whether the type or amount of milk and dairy fat should be adjusted — this isn''t something to decide on general low-fat instinct alone. Seek advice if your child has persistent poor growth, chronic diarrhea, repeatedly greasy or unusually pale stools, or a diagnosed condition affecting digestion or fat absorption. Don''t simply add more oil or ghee without identifying the cause. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s food doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Continue including appropriate amounts of fat in family meals, using a mixture of cooking oils and moderate amounts of ghee or butter, keep whole milk (or an appropriate alternative) as part of your child''s diet until age two, and save the low-fat conversation for your child''s doctor if a specific risk factor applies.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. Cook them all the way through, keep chicken boneless and finely shredded, and keep fish deboned and finely flaked; the two fish choices this week are alternatives, not a requirement, so use the chicken, egg or vegetarian option instead if you''d rather.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Fat is an essential part of a toddler''s diet, not something to cut back on. It''s a concentrated source of energy for a stomach that''s still small, it''s needed to absorb vitamins A, D, E and K from food, and it supports the brain development that''s happening rapidly at this age. Pediatric guidance is direct about this: a low-fat diet isn''t the healthy choice for a toddler the way it might be marketed to adults, and cutting fat too far can mean a child doesn''t get enough calories overall, not just less fat. Experts generally place a toddler''s fat intake somewhere in the low-to-mid 30s percent of daily calories — higher than what''s often recommended for adults — though the exact figure varies a little by source.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha cooked with a modest amount of ghee or oil", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha cooked with a modest amount of ghee or oil, with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal, cooked with its usual tempering, and soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin, the dal swapped for the chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, yolk included, chopped into manageable pieces", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a small amount of ghee or oil, if not already used in cooking", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild fish curry, Indian or Atlantic mackerel — not king mackerel — deboned and finely flaked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Whole milk until two", "notes": "If your child drinks cow''s milk, pasteurized, unsweetened whole milk is commonly recommended from 12 to 24 months — the fat is considered part of the point, not a drawback. Breastfeeding can continue for as long as parent and child wish, and cow''s milk isn''t required when nutritional needs are already being met through breastfeeding and a varied diet; milk, curd and cheese all count toward dairy intake, so more milk on top of that isn''t automatically better, and a lot of milk can crowd out iron-rich food. For a dairy alternative, choose an unsweetened product fortified with calcium and vitamin D that suits your child''s age — not all plant drinks are nutritionally equivalent to cow''s milk. If your child has had excessive weight gain, or there''s a family history of obesity, high cholesterol or heart disease, ask your child''s doctor whether an earlier switch to a lower-fat milk makes sense; some newer research questions whether switching early actually helps, so treat this as a conversation for your child''s doctor rather than a decision to make on general low-fat instinct alone.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia porridge cooked in whole milk", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia porridge cooked in whole milk with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a rajma curry, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken in a mild gravy", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed ripe banana with smooth peanut or sesame paste, only if previously introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "Full-fat curd with grated paneer", "dinner_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked carrot", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrot", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of rajma or dal with soft roti"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Which fats to lean on", "notes": "Not all fats need equal attention. Unsaturated fats — found in cooking oils like groundnut, sesame or sunflower, in oily fish, and in nuts once they''re safely introduced — are the preferred everyday sources for a toddler. Saturated fat, found in ghee, butter and full-fat dairy, doesn''t need to be avoided, but is usually kept to a moderate share of the day''s food rather than the dominant one. Trans fat is the one type worth actively avoiding at any age. Avoid vanaspati and products listing partially hydrogenated oils. Packaged and repeatedly deep-fried foods are also best limited, but their fat composition varies, so check the ingredient list rather than assuming every packaged food contains trans fat.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma prepared with a modest amount of groundnut oil", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked lauki", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed avocado", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with crumbled paneer stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild egg curry, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Fats and brain development", "notes": "Oily fish provides the long-chain omega-3 fats DHA and EPA, which are closely linked with brain and eye development — a window of growth that continues through the toddler years, not just infancy. Walnuts, ground flaxseed and some oils provide a different omega-3 called ALA, which the body converts to DHA only in limited amounts; these vegetarian foods remain nutritious, but they aren''t a direct substitute for the DHA and EPA that oily fish provides. None of this calls for a special supplement in an otherwise varied diet — it''s more a reason to let oily fish show up in the week''s meals a couple of times where that fits your family''s diet.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with a smooth, thinned peanut chutney with no whole peanut pieces, only if peanut has already been introduced safely", "breakfast_b": "Idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, mashed and mixed in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked yellow pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and green peas, mashed until very soft", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with finely ground walnut stirred in, only if walnut has already been introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe papaya", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild pomfret curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal on the side"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "The \"low-fat is healthier\" myth", "notes": "It''s an easy habit to slip into — measuring a toddler''s food against adult low-fat advice, or cutting fat further because that''s the health message the rest of the family follows. Restricting fat too aggressively can reduce the energy density of a toddler''s diet. Some commercially marketed low-fat foods may also contain added sugar, so it''s worth assessing the whole product rather than assuming \"low-fat\" automatically means healthier. Include a variety of fats in age-appropriate amounts, rather than either cutting them out or adding extra on top of what a dish would normally have.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla with a mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dhokla with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked beetroot", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked sweet potato mashed with thick curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong-masoor dal, prepared with its usual modest amount of oil or ghee", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild boneless chicken keema, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A gentle habit, not a rule", "notes": "There''s no need to track fat grams or percentages day to day. Include a variety of fats in age-appropriate amounts — a mix of cooking oils, moderate ghee or butter, and full-fat dairy — rather than either restricting fat or adding extra on top of what a dish would normally have. The one time this is worth a proper conversation with your child''s doctor is if there''s unusually rapid weight gain, a family history of obesity, high cholesterol or heart disease, or signs affecting your child''s growth or digestion; otherwise, this is one part of feeding your toddler that mostly takes care of itself. A mix of cooking oils, ghee used the way it normally is in a dish, and oily fish where that fits your family''s diet a couple of times a week — that''s really the whole approach, and none of it calls for special extra measures once fat is included at ordinary amounts.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge cooked with its usual amount of ghee or oil", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked brinjal", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked tinda", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed guava pulp, thoroughly deseeded", "nourishment_break_b": "A fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces, mixed into a small bowl of dal", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild, boneless chicken curry, finely shredded", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal or an extra serving of the main meal"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Has your family been applying adult low-fat habits to your toddler''s food, and is there a small adjustment — restoring an appropriate cooking oil, nut or seed paste, full-fat dairy, or another suitable fat source — worth making?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   '"Healthy" doesn''t mean the same thing at every age — a toddler''s need for fat is one of the clearest examples of that, and it''s easy to forget in a household that''s otherwise watching its own diet.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
