  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 92: How much water does your toddler need?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   92,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much water does your toddler need?',
   '"Plain water is the everyday drink to offer between meals and alongside food — make it readily available, follow your child''s thirst, and avoid forcing a fixed amount."',
   'Children aged one to three commonly need around four cups of total fluid a day, including water and milk, although needs rise with heat, activity, fever, vomiting or diarrhea. Beverage guidance for 12-24 months also describes a broad range of approximately 1-4 cups of plain water and 2-3 cups of milk, but these are not amounts to add together at their maximums — where your child falls within each range depends on breastfeeding, other beverages, food moisture, weather and activity. If cow''s milk is part of your child''s diet, approximately 2 cups of fluid milk a day is sufficient for many toddlers. Curd and cheese contribute to dairy nutrition and may reduce how much milk is needed, but they shouldn''t be counted as equivalent cups of drinking fluid. Breastfeeding and the rest of your child''s diet also affect individual needs. Indian dietary guidelines don''t publish a toddler-specific water-by-age table the way some other guidance does, so these ranges are the most granular figures available. Don''t routinely withhold water from a thirsty child, but there''s also no need to encourage continuous drinking beyond thirst. Small sips of water with meals are appropriate and generally don''t interfere with eating. Avoid pressuring your child to finish a large drink immediately before a meal, since a large volume may temporarily reduce appetite — a modest amount offered with the meal is a better habit than a big glass finished just beforehand. Make water easy to access, but keep drinking seated: don''t allow your toddler to walk, run or climb with a bottle, spouted cup, straw or open cup in their mouth. Knowing the signs of good hydration is more useful day to day than counting cups. Reassuring signs include regular urination for your child, a moist mouth, tears when crying, normal alertness and usual play. If you notice a mild change such as darker urine or a slightly dry mouth, offer fluid and watch how your child responds. Seek prompt medical advice when several dehydration signs occur together, symptoms persist or worsen, your child drinks poorly, has very little or no urine, is unusually sleepy, or can''t keep fluids down — these become more likely during vomiting, diarrhea, fever or hot weather, when fluid needs rise and losses can happen faster than usual. Water is the default drink between milk feeds. Whole fruit is preferable to juice, and juice is unnecessary for most toddlers; if 100% juice is offered, keep it to no more than about 4 ounces (about half a cup) a day for this age and serve it with a meal. Sweetened drinks, sports drinks, energy drinks and anything caffeinated have no place in a toddler''s routine at any amount — this isn''t about moderation for these particular drinks, it''s a straightforward avoid. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below (for diabetes, blood pressure or thyroid medication) apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Several meals this week include naturally water-rich foods (cucumber, watermelon, orange, papaya) alongside the usual choices — there''s no need to track this separately from your child''s regular water offered through the day.',
   'If your child has significant diarrhea or repeated vomiting: use a commercial oral rehydration solution prepared or used exactly according to the label instructions, unless your child''s clinician advises otherwise — offer small, frequent sips and continue breastfeeding and age-appropriate food as tolerated. Plain water, juice, coconut water and sports drinks are not substitutes for ORS when significant fluid and electrolyte losses are occurring. Seek urgent medical care if your child can''t keep fluids down, is difficult to wake, has very little or no urine, has a very dry mouth or sunken eyes, breathes unusually fast, has blood in the stool or vomit, or appears progressively worse. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s fluids doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Offer water regularly through the day, keep milk to its usual amount, keep drinking seated, and offer a bit more fluid during hot weather or illness.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. Cook them all the way through, keep chicken boneless and finely shredded, and keep fish deboned and finely flaked; the two fish choices this week are alternatives, not a requirement, so use the chicken, egg or vegetarian option instead if you''d rather.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Children aged one to three commonly need around four cups of total fluid a day, including water and milk, although needs rise with heat, activity, fever, vomiting or diarrhea. Beverage guidance for 12-24 months also describes a broad range of approximately 1-4 cups of plain water and 2-3 cups of milk, but these are not amounts to add together at their maximums — where your child falls within each range depends on breastfeeding, other beverages, food moisture, weather and activity. If cow''s milk is part of your child''s diet, approximately 2 cups of fluid milk a day is sufficient for many toddlers. Curd and cheese contribute to dairy nutrition and may reduce how much milk is needed, but they shouldn''t be counted as equivalent cups of drinking fluid. Breastfeeding and the rest of your child''s diet also affect individual needs. Indian dietary guidelines don''t publish a toddler-specific water-by-age table the way some other guidance does, so the ranges here are the most granular figures available — use them as a general sense of scale, not a strict daily count.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with a mild sambar", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum", "nourishment_break_a": "Peeled cucumber, finely grated into thick curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal and soft-cooked lauki", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild sardine curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Water around meals", "notes": "Small sips of water with meals are appropriate and generally don''t interfere with eating. Avoid pressuring your child to finish a large drink immediately before a meal, since a large volume may temporarily reduce appetite — a modest amount offered with the meal is a better habit than a big glass finished just beforehand. For your child: Make water easy to access, but keep drinking seated. Don''t allow your toddler to walk, run or climb with a bottle, spouted cup, straw or open cup in their mouth — offer water regularly rather than having your child carry a cup around.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with a mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage", "nourishment_break_a": "Watermelon, seeds removed, cut into small, soft pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana and a pinch of cumin", "dinner_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked beetroot", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked beetroot", "still_hungry": "An extra spoon of dal on the side"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Recognizing thirst and hydration", "notes": "Knowing the signs of good hydration is more useful day to day than counting cups. Reassuring signs include regular urination for your child, a moist mouth, tears when crying, normal alertness and usual play. If you notice a mild change such as darker urine or a slightly dry mouth, offer fluid and watch how your child responds. Seek prompt medical advice when several dehydration signs occur together, symptoms persist or worsen, your child drinks poorly, has very little or no urine, is unusually sleepy, or can''t keep fluids down — these become more likely during vomiting, diarrhea, fever or hot weather, when fluid needs rise and losses can happen faster than usual.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with mashed ripe banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "nourishment_break_a": "Orange flesh, seeds and tough membranes removed, cut into small, soft pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and mashed banana", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild chicken curry, boneless and finely shredded", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What to skip", "notes": "Water is the default drink between milk feeds. Whole fruit is preferable to juice, and juice is unnecessary for most toddlers; if 100% juice is offered, keep it to no more than about 4 ounces (about half a cup) a day for this age and serve it with a meal. Sweetened drinks, sports drinks, energy drinks and anything caffeinated have no place in a toddler''s routine at any amount — this isn''t about moderation for these particular drinks, it''s a straightforward avoid.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa with a mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dosa with a fully cooked egg bhurji mixed into the filling", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked cauliflower", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe mango", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a toor dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild egg bhurji stirred in, fully cooked", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Heat, travel and safe water", "notes": "Fluid needs rise in hot weather and during travel, so offer water a little more often on warm days and before and after outdoor play. For drinking water itself, boiling handles microbial risk but not dissolved chemical contaminants, so use whichever source or treatment method your family already trusts as reliably safe — the same standard that applies to the rest of your child''s food and drink. When traveling, use water from a trusted source — such as an intact, factory-sealed bottle from a reliable supplier, or water treated through a method appropriate to the local risk — and avoid ice when its water source or handling is uncertain.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a squeeze of lemon", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked french beans", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft ripe papaya with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and soft-cooked lauki", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild salmon curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A gentle habit, not a rule", "notes": "There''s no need to track cups or enforce a fixed water schedule. Offering water regularly through the day, alongside meals and snacks, and a little extra attention during illness or heat cover what matters. Most toddlers are reasonably good at drinking to thirst when water is offered — there''s no need to encourage drinking beyond that, and water is still best had seated rather than while walking, running or climbing. Water offered regularly through the day, milk kept to its usual amount, and a bit more offered on hot days or when your child is unwell — that''s really the whole approach, and none of it needs close tracking.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with a mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Idli with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked brinjal", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked brinjal", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked sweet potato mashed with a small bowl of curd on the side", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely grated paneer and a pinch of cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mild boneless chicken curry, lightly spiced", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Is water offered regularly between meals while your toddler is seated, and would you recognize the early signs if intake ever fell a little short?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Thirst is one of the body''s useful signals, even in a toddler who can''t yet say the word — offering water regularly while your child is seated usually matters more than watching the clock.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 93: Fiber in your toddler's diet — how much, and why not too much
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   93,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Fiber in your toddler''s diet — how much, and why not too much',
   '"Whole grains, dal, vegetables and fruit already give your toddler fiber without any extra effort — bring them in gradually, offer water regularly while your toddler is seated, and there''s no routine need for bran, husk or a fiber supplement unless a doctor advises one for a specific problem."',
   'Fiber is the part of plant foods your body doesn''t fully digest, and it plays a genuinely useful role even this early — it helps keep digestion moving and adds bulk that supports regular, comfortable stools. Some of the longer-term benefits linked to fiber, such as effects on cholesterol and blood sugar, come mostly from adult research and aren''t something to expect or track in a toddler; the benefit that matters most at this age is steady digestion. A commonly used Adequate Intake for children aged one to three is 19 grams of fiber a day — but this is a population reference based on limited direct evidence in very young children, not a number to calculate or hit precisely every day. A varied pattern of fruit, vegetables, pulses and grains matters more than counting grams. Many families, including many in India, can meet their toddler''s fiber needs through ordinary foods already used at home — everyday dal, vegetables, fruit and a mix of grains — and this week is mainly about keeping that steady rather than chasing a number. Whole grains can be offered regularly, but every grain doesn''t need to be whole grain. Some guidance recommends making at least half of grain choices whole grain — not switching everything over. Indian guidance on millets is a separate, additional point: it suggests millets may form a portion of a child''s cereal intake, not a target for the whole-grain share overall. A practical mix — rice, wheat, oats and millets, offered according to your child''s appetite, tolerance and your family''s usual diet — serves a toddler better than an all-or-nothing switch. More fiber isn''t automatically better at this age the way it often is for adults. A very high-fiber, high-bulk meal can fill a toddler up before they''ve taken in enough calories, which matters more for a toddler than an adult given how much they need to eat relative to their size. Whole grains and pulses also contain naturally occurring phytates, which can reduce the absorption of some minerals — soaking, sprouting, fermenting and cooking can reduce phytate levels, and in a varied diet that also includes iron-, zinc- and calcium-rich foods, there''s no need to avoid these foods or calculate phytate exposure. If your toddler is a light eater or gaining weight slowly, it''s more useful to keep meals calorie-dense — usual amounts of ghee, oil, dal, dairy and protein alongside fiber-rich foods — rather than leaning further into bulky, high-fiber options. If you''re adding more whole grains, legumes or fruit than your toddler is used to, bring them in gradually over a few weeks rather than all at once, and offer water regularly while your toddler is seated — fiber and fluid work together. Reconsider the overall pattern if your child develops persistent diarrhea, significant bloating or pain, becomes full so quickly that regular food intake falls, has worsening constipation, or isn''t growing as expected. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Several meals this week lean on whole grains, dal and fruit as they naturally are — there''s no need to add fiber on top of what''s already in the dish.',
   'If your child has ongoing loose stools, reduced appetite or slow weight gain: mention this to your child''s doctor. Large amounts of fruit juice, a very high-fiber pattern and an inadequately balanced diet may contribute to loose stools in some toddlers. Limit juice, maintain a balanced diet with adequate dietary fat, and seek assessment if loose stools persist or affect appetite or growth. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s fiber intake doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Offer dal, vegetables, fruit and a mix of grains through the week, bring in anything new gradually, and offer water regularly while your toddler is seated.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. Cook them all the way through, keep chicken boneless and finely shredded, and keep fish deboned and finely flaked; the two fish choices this week (salmon and pomfret) are alternatives, not a requirement, so use the chicken, egg or vegetarian option instead if you''d rather.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Fiber is the part of plant foods your body doesn''t fully digest, and it plays a genuinely useful role even this early — it helps keep digestion moving and adds bulk that supports regular, comfortable stools. Some of the longer-term benefits linked to fiber, such as effects on cholesterol and blood sugar, come mostly from adult research and aren''t something to expect or track in a toddler; the benefit that matters most at this age is steady digestion. A commonly used Adequate Intake for children aged one to three is 19 grams of fiber a day — but this is a population reference based on limited direct evidence in very young children, not a number to calculate or hit precisely every day. A varied pattern of fruit, vegetables, pulses and grains matters more than counting grams. Many families, including many in India, can meet their toddler''s fiber needs through ordinary foods already used at home — everyday dal, vegetables, fruit and a mix of grains — and this week is mainly about keeping that steady rather than chasing a number. Worth knowing: Fruit and vegetable skins add some fiber, but safety depends on the fruit''s firmness and how it''s prepared. For a young toddler, peel firm fruits such as apple or pear and serve them soft-cooked and mashed or very finely grated, while closely supervised.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats porridge cooked in whole milk", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats porridge with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "Peeled, very finely grated apple stirred into thick curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana and finely ground flaxseed, only if flaxseed has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild salmon curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked bhindi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday sources", "notes": "Fiber shows up naturally across an ordinary Indian toddler thali — dal of any kind, vegetables, fruit, and grains such as wheat, ragi, jowar, bajra and oats. Pulses are a particularly concentrated source, and whole grains carry noticeably more fiber than their refined counterparts. None of this needs a special shopping list or a supplement — the same dal-roti-sabzi-fruit pattern already used through this series covers it, offered across the day rather than concentrated into one large serving.", "breakfast_a": "Jowar dosa with a mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Jowar dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and cluster beans, cooked until very soft and finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and cluster beans, cooked until very soft and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe chikoo pulp, thoroughly deseeded", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with finely grated paneer and a pinch of jeera", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a toor dal and soft-cooked carrot", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with crumbled paneer and soft-cooked carrot", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Whole grains without going all-in", "notes": "Whole grains can be offered regularly, but every grain doesn''t need to be whole grain. Some guidance recommends making at least half of grain choices whole grain — not switching everything over. Indian guidance on millets is a separate, additional point: it suggests millets may form a portion of a child''s cereal intake, not a target for the whole-grain share overall. A practical mix — rice, wheat, oats and millets, offered according to your child''s appetite, tolerance and your family''s usual diet — serves a toddler better than an all-or-nothing switch.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma made with equal parts suji and broken wheat", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma made with equal parts suji and broken wheat, with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked carrot", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked pear, peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe papaya and a pinch of cumin", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked tinda", "dinner_b": "Rice with a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked tinda", "still_hungry": "An extra soft roti"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why not to overdo it", "notes": "More fiber isn''t automatically better at this age the way it often is for adults. A very high-fiber, high-bulk meal can fill a toddler up before they''ve taken in enough calories, which matters more for a toddler than an adult given how much they need to eat relative to their size. Whole grains and pulses also contain naturally occurring phytates, which can reduce the absorption of some minerals — soaking, sprouting, fermenting and cooking can reduce phytate levels, and in a varied diet that also includes iron-, zinc- and calcium-rich foods, there''s no need to avoid these foods or calculate phytate exposure. If your toddler is a light eater or gaining weight slowly, it''s more useful to keep meals calorie-dense — usual amounts of ghee, oil, dal, dairy and protein alongside fiber-rich foods — rather than leaning further into bulky, high-fiber options.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a toor dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a mild boneless chicken curry, finely shredded, and soft-cooked capsicum", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft-cooked sweet potato mashed with a small bowl of dal on the side", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely ground almond stirred in, only if almond has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild pomfret curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked ridge gourd", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Introducing fiber gradually", "notes": "If you''re adding more whole grains, legumes or fruit than your toddler is used to, bring them in gradually over a few weeks rather than all at once, and offer water regularly while your toddler is seated — fiber and fluid work together. Reconsider the overall pattern if your child develops persistent diarrhea, significant bloating or pain, becomes full so quickly that regular food intake falls, has worsening constipation, or isn''t growing as expected. For your child: Offer water regularly while your toddler is seated as you bring in more whole grains, dal or fruit than usual this week — fiber and fluid work together.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia porridge with mashed ripe banana", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia porridge with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, chopped into manageable pieces", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe pear, peeled and cooked until soft", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with crumbled paneer and a pinch of ajwain", "dinner_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked bhindi", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild egg curry, fully cooked, and soft-cooked bhindi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A gentle habit, not a rule", "notes": "There''s no need to count grams or plan meals around a fiber target. Serving dal, vegetables, fruit and a mix of grains through the week the way this series already recommends covers it without extra effort. Bran, psyllium husk and fiber supplements shouldn''t be added routinely to a toddler''s food — use a pediatric fiber product only if your child''s doctor advises one for a specific problem. A varied plate through the week, water offered regularly while your toddler is seated, and no deliberate effort to maximize fiber in any one dish — that''s really the whole approach.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi idli with a mild sambar", "breakfast_b": "Ragi idli with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked french beans", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked french beans", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe papaya and a pinch of jeera", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, mashed", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked carrot", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked carrot", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Are whole grains, dal, vegetables and fruit already covering fiber through the week without your having to add anything extra, and is water still being offered regularly while your toddler is seated?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Fiber is one more thing your toddler''s ordinary thali is probably already getting right — steady, varied meals do more here than any deliberate effort to add more.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
