-- postpartum week 94: How much protein does your toddler actually need?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   94,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much protein does your toddler actually need?',
   '"Many toddlers can meet their protein needs through ordinary family foods such as dal, beans, milk or curd, grains, eggs, fish or meat. There is usually no need to count grams or add a protein powder — but adequacy depends on how much and how variedly the child actually eats."',
   'Protein needs at this age are smaller than most parents expect. A commonly used reference for children aged one to three is about 13 grams of protein per day, although an individual child''s needs vary with body size and overall health — India''s own dietary guidelines land in a very similar range. Milk or curd, dal or beans, grains and other family foods can contribute across the day. Many toddlers eating adequate amounts of a varied diet can meet this reference without protein powders or deliberate gram-counting, including on a well-planned vegetarian diet. However, intake should not be assumed to be adequate in a child who eats very little, avoids several food groups, follows a poorly planned restrictive diet, or is not growing as expected. Once nutritional needs are met, deliberately pushing extra protein has not been shown to improve a healthy toddler''s growth. Some infant-formula studies have associated higher-protein formulas with faster early weight gain, but that evidence should not be directly applied to ordinary toddler foods — an extra portion of dal, curd or egg is not the same thing as concentrated formula protein in early infancy. The practical message is simple: offer balanced meals and respond to appetite rather than adding concentrated protein merely to reach a higher number. Protein powders are not routinely needed for healthy toddlers and may displace ordinary foods or provide unnecessary added sugars, sweeteners or nutrients. "Growing-up" and toddler milks generally offer no special advantage for most children over an appropriate varied diet. They should not be used as substitutes for a proper assessment when eating or growth is a concern. If your toddler eats vegetarian, you don''t need to carefully pair foods at the same meal to get "complete" protein — that idea was common decades ago but has since been dropped by the nutrition bodies that once taught it. A varied diet across the day, the kind already built into everyday Indian eating — dal with rice or roti, milk, curd — covers this without any deliberate combining. Legumes, dairy and grains each bring something a little different, and eating a mix of them through the week is what actually matters, not matching them up on the same plate. A vegan diet requires more deliberate planning than a vegetarian diet that includes dairy or eggs. It should include varied pulses, beans, tofu or other soy foods, grains, nut or seed pastes prepared safely, and appropriate fortified foods. Vitamin B12 requires particular attention, and a suitable supplement may be necessary. If your family follows a vegan diet, discuss the child''s overall food and supplementation plan with a qualified pediatric professional. Vegetarian diets that include dairy or eggs still need variety, but protein foods do not have to be deliberately paired at every meal. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Several meals this week naturally include a mix of dal, dairy, egg or meat — there''s no need to add protein on top of what''s already in the dish.',
   'If your child is a very restrictive eater or gaining weight slowly: mention this to your child''s doctor. A restrictive eater may still obtain protein from a limited number of accepted foods, but adequacy cannot be assumed from the presence of milk or grains alone. The child''s total intake, dietary variety, growth and possible deficiencies should be considered together — don''t start a protein powder as a substitute for that assessment. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s protein intake doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Offer your toddler''s usual mix of milk, dal, grains and vegetables, and there''s no reason to track protein separately or add a supplement on top.',
   'Where a meat, fish or egg option appears, it is optional — choose the vegetarian alternative freely. Cook them all the way through, keep chicken boneless and finely shredded, and keep fish deboned and finely flaked; the two fish choices this week (tilapia and pomfret) are alternatives, not a requirement, so use the chicken, egg or vegetarian option instead if you''d rather.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Protein needs at this age are smaller than most parents expect. A commonly used reference for children aged one to three is about 13 grams of protein per day, although an individual child''s needs vary with body size and overall health — India''s own dietary guidelines land in a very similar range. Milk or curd, dal or beans, grains and other family foods can contribute across the day. Many toddlers eating adequate amounts of a varied diet can meet this reference without protein powders or deliberate gram-counting, including on a well-planned vegetarian diet. However, intake should not be assumed to be adequate in a child who eats very little, avoids several food groups, follows a poorly planned restrictive diet, or is not growing as expected.", "breakfast_a": "Plain dosa with a mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Plain dosa with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked cabbage", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild tilapia curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pumpkin", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday sources", "notes": "Protein turns up in more places than the obvious \"protein foods.\" A cup of milk gives about 8 grams, a small bowl of dal 4 to 8 grams depending on how it''s made, an egg about 6 grams, and even roti or rice contribute a couple of grams each. Paneer, curd, chicken and fish add more if you''re using them, but they''re not what makes the difference between enough and not enough — an everyday combination of dairy or another suitable alternative, pulses, grains and varied family foods can contribute enough when the child eats adequate portions across the day.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with a mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a toor dal and soft-cooked beetroot", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked beetroot", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and a pinch of pepper", "nourishment_break_b": "Thick curd with a fully cooked egg, finely mashed", "dinner_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild egg curry, fully cooked, and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Why more isn''t better", "notes": "Once nutritional needs are met, deliberately pushing extra protein has not been shown to improve a healthy toddler''s growth. Some infant-formula studies have associated higher-protein formulas with faster early weight gain, but that evidence should not be directly applied to ordinary toddler foods — an extra portion of dal, curd or egg is not the same thing as concentrated formula protein in early infancy. The practical message is simple: offer balanced meals and respond to appetite rather than adding concentrated protein merely to reach a higher number.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a fully cooked egg bhurji mixed in", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bhindi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with orange segments, seeds and membranes removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Moong sprouts cooked thoroughly until soft, then lightly mashed with a pinch of jeera", "dinner_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked spinach", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Powders and toddler milks aren''t needed", "notes": "Protein powders are not routinely needed for healthy toddlers and may displace ordinary foods or provide unnecessary added sugars, sweeteners or nutrients. \"Growing-up\" and toddler milks generally offer no special advantage for most children over an appropriate varied diet. They should not be used as substitutes for a proper assessment when eating or growth is a concern.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge cooked in whole milk", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge cooked in whole milk with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked lauki", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked lauki", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with finely grated paneer and a pinch of turmeric", "dinner_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked green beans", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild pomfret curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked green beans", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Vegetarian protein, without the myth", "notes": "If your toddler eats vegetarian, you don''t need to carefully pair foods at the same meal to get \"complete\" protein — that idea was common decades ago but has since been dropped by the nutrition bodies that once taught it. A varied diet across the day, the kind already built into everyday Indian eating — dal with rice or roti, milk, curd — covers this without any deliberate combining. Legumes, dairy and grains each bring something a little different, and eating a mix of them through the week is what actually matters, not matching them up on the same plate. A vegan diet requires more deliberate planning than a vegetarian diet that includes dairy or eggs. It should include varied pulses, beans, tofu or other soy foods, grains, nut or seed pastes prepared safely, and appropriate fortified foods. Vitamin B12 requires particular attention, and a suitable supplement may be necessary. If your family follows a vegan diet, discuss the child''s overall food and supplementation plan with a qualified pediatric professional. Vegetarian diets that include dairy or eggs still need variety, but protein foods do not have to be deliberately paired at every meal.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with a mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry, beans cooked until very soft and lightly mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked sweet corn", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana and finely ground sesame, only if sesame has already been introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely mashed", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong-masoor dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A gentle habit, not a rule", "notes": "There''s no need to track protein grams or add anything extra to what your toddler already eats. If your child is a very restrictive eater or gaining weight slowly, that''s worth raising with your doctor — the fix is a proper look at your child''s overall eating pattern and growth, not a protein powder or supplement added on your own. Dairy or a suitable alternative, pulses, grains and whichever egg, fish, meat or other protein foods your family uses can meet protein needs as part of a varied diet, without routine gram-counting or protein products.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with a small bowl of curd on the side", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe pear or banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely ground pumpkin seed stirred in, only if pumpkin seed has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked capsicum", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, is protein something that was already being covered by ordinary meals without your having to think about it?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Protein is one of the quieter reassurances in toddler feeding — a varied diet commonly covers it without separate tracking or special protein products.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 95: How much calcium does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   95,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much calcium does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Many toddlers can meet their calcium needs through ordinary foods — milk, curd, paneer, ragi, sesame and leafy greens all contribute. There''s usually no need for a supplement, but a child who avoids dairy altogether needs a bit more deliberate planning."',
   'Calcium reference values differ between national frameworks. Indian guidance may use approximately 500 milligrams per day for this age, while some international frameworks use 700 milligrams for children aged one to three. These are population reference values, not numbers families need to calculate meal by meal — the practical takeaway is to follow the Indian reference consistently while emphasizing varied calcium-containing foods rather than daily counting. Young children can absorb calcium efficiently, but the amount absorbed varies according to the food source, vitamin D status and the rest of the diet — a steady pattern of suitable calcium-containing foods matters more than estimating an absorption percentage. Milk, curd, paneer, calcium-set tofu, fortified alternatives and selected grains or greens can all contribute; whether the total is adequate depends on the foods chosen, their portions, and how much the child actually consumes. If your toddler has little or no dairy, several other foods carry meaningful calcium. Sesame can contribute calcium, but its calcium content varies considerably between hulled and unhulled forms. For a toddler, use sesame finely ground into a smooth paste or powder and stir a small amount into soft food, only once sesame has been introduced safely — don''t offer whole seeds or firm, sticky sesame laddoos as a way to add calcium, since both carry a choking risk at this age. Moringa or methi leaves also contribute. If dairy is excluded, calcium-set tofu (check the label for calcium sulfate or calcium chloride) and an age-appropriate fortified alternative can help — check the label for calcium, vitamin D, protein and added sugar, since fortification and suitability vary considerably between soy, almond, oat and other plant milks. Homemade nut or plant milk isn''t fortified and shouldn''t be assumed to replace milk nutritionally. Choosing a primary milk alternative for a child under two, especially with a cow''s milk allergy or a vegan diet, is worth discussing individually with your doctor. Certain foods, particularly spinach, contain compounds called oxalates that reduce how much of their own calcium the body actually absorbs — spinach''s calcium is poorly absorbed for this reason. Some grains, pulses, nuts and seeds also contain phytates, which can reduce mineral absorption; soaking, sprouting, fermenting grain or pulse batters, and cooking can reduce phytate levels. Curd is itself a calcium-containing fermented food, but it doesn''t remove phytates from unrelated foods served alongside it. In an ordinary varied diet, where spinach or dal is one of several calcium sources rather than the main one, this is a minor detail rather than something to plan around; it mainly matters if a narrow diet leans heavily on a single high-oxalate or high-phytate food. Many toddlers can meet their calcium needs through a varied diet containing suitable calcium sources, and a supplement is not routinely necessary. However, adequacy depends on the foods offered, their portions and how much the child consumes. The conversation is different for a child who genuinely can''t get enough from food — significant dairy avoidance from a cow''s milk allergy, a vegan diet without deliberate planning, or another very limited diet. If dairy and fortified alternatives are absent, the child eats a very limited range of foods, or growth and bone health are concerns, review the overall diet with the child''s clinician before starting a supplement. More isn''t better once needs are met, either — going well beyond the reference through food alone is genuinely difficult, but stacking a supplement on top of an already-adequate diet doesn''t add benefit and isn''t something to do without medical guidance. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and any medical-condition notes below apply to her, not the toddler — "if you''re still hungry" also refers to the mother. Several meals naturally include ragi, sesame, paneer or leafy greens; there''s no need to add calcium on top.',
   'If your child avoids dairy altogether: mention this to your child''s doctor, especially for a cow''s milk allergy, a vegan diet, or another reason dairy is fully excluded. Lactose intolerance alone usually still allows curd and hard cheese — but full dairy avoidance calls for a deliberate look at non-dairy sources like ragi, sesame, calcium-set tofu, leafy greens and fortified plant milk, and possibly a supplement planned with your doctor. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — this week''s focus on your child''s calcium intake doesn''t change what you do for yourself. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be complicated. Offer your toddler''s usual mix of milk, curd, paneer, ragi and vegetables, and there''s no reason to track calcium separately or add a supplement on top unless your doctor suggests it.',
   'Where a meat, fish or egg option appears, it is optional — choose the vegetarian alternative freely. Cook them all the way through, keep chicken boneless and finely shredded, and keep fish deboned and finely flaked; this week''s fish choice (basa) is optional too, so use chicken, egg or a vegetarian option instead if you''d rather.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Calcium reference values differ between national frameworks. Indian guidance may use approximately 500 milligrams per day for this age, while some international frameworks use 700 milligrams for children aged one to three. These are population reference values, not numbers families need to calculate meal by meal — the practical takeaway is to follow the Indian reference consistently while emphasizing varied calcium-containing foods rather than daily counting. Young children can absorb calcium efficiently, but the amount absorbed varies according to the food source, vitamin D status and the rest of the diet — a steady pattern of suitable calcium-containing foods matters more than estimating an absorption percentage. Milk, curd, paneer, calcium-set tofu, fortified alternatives and selected grains or greens can all contribute; whether the total is adequate depends on the foods chosen, their portions, and how much the child actually consumes.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek) leaves", "lunch_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek) leaves", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and a pinch of cumin", "nourishment_break_b": "Thick curd with a small amount of finely ground sesame stirred through, only if sesame has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and soft-cooked pumpkin", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mild basa curry, boneless, flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pumpkin", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday sources: milk, curd, paneer and ragi", "notes": "Milk, curd and paneer are the most familiar calcium sources, and they work well: approximately 240 ml of milk provides around 300 milligrams of calcium, although values vary by product, and calcium in curd and paneer also varies with portion size and preparation. Less familiar but useful is ragi — it contains more calcium than many other grains, though how much it actually contributes depends on the amount of dry ragi used and how it''s prepared, so it''s best thought of as one part of a varied calcium pattern rather than a direct substitute for a cup of milk. Ragi porridge and ragi dosa are already common toddler foods across India for good reason, alongside milk, curd and paneer, not instead of them. More milk isn''t automatically better, either. Large volumes can fill up a toddler and reduce appetite for other foods, and have been linked to lower iron intake when milk crowds out iron-rich foods. Offer milk as part of a varied plate rather than using it to replace meals.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge cooked in whole milk with mashed ripe banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge cooked in whole milk with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a toor dal and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked drumstick, pulp only", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with grated paneer and a pinch of ajwain", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of hing", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked amaranth leaves (chaulai)", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked amaranth leaves (chaulai)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Non-dairy sources", "notes": "If your toddler has little or no dairy, several other foods carry meaningful calcium. Sesame can contribute calcium, but its calcium content varies considerably between hulled and unhulled forms. For a toddler, use sesame finely ground into a smooth paste or powder and stir a small amount into soft food, only once sesame has been introduced safely — don''t offer whole seeds or firm, sticky sesame laddoos as a way to add calcium, since both carry a choking risk at this age. Moringa or methi leaves also contribute. If dairy is excluded, calcium-set tofu (check the label for calcium sulfate or calcium chloride) and an age-appropriate fortified alternative can help — check the label for calcium, vitamin D, protein and added sugar, since fortification and suitability vary considerably between soy, almond, oat and other plant milks. Homemade nut or plant milk isn''t fortified and shouldn''t be assumed to replace milk nutritionally. Choosing a primary milk alternative for a child under two, especially with a cow''s milk allergy or a vegan diet, is worth discussing individually with your doctor.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla with a mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dhokla with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal and soft-cooked moringa (drumstick) leaves", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked moringa (drumstick) leaves", "nourishment_break_a": "Calcium-set tofu mashed smoothly with soft-cooked vegetables or mashed ripe fruit", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with finely ground sesame, only if sesame has already been introduced safely", "dinner_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked capsicum", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mild egg curry, fully cooked, and soft-cooked capsicum", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A word on spinach", "notes": "Certain foods, particularly spinach, contain compounds called oxalates that reduce how much of their own calcium the body actually absorbs — spinach''s calcium is poorly absorbed for this reason. Some grains, pulses, nuts and seeds also contain phytates, which can reduce mineral absorption; soaking, sprouting, fermenting grain or pulse batters, and cooking can reduce phytate levels. Curd is itself a calcium-containing fermented food, but it doesn''t remove phytates from unrelated foods served alongside it. In an ordinary varied diet, where spinach or dal is one of several calcium sources rather than the main one, this is a minor detail rather than something to plan around; it mainly matters if a narrow diet leans heavily on a single high-oxalate or high-phytate food.", "breakfast_a": "Rava idli with a mild sambar", "breakfast_b": "Rava idli with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked spinach", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked spinach", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe banana and a pinch of turmeric", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with crumbled paneer stirred in", "dinner_a": "Rice with a toor dal and soft-cooked cauliflower", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Supplements aren''t the default", "notes": "Many toddlers can meet their calcium needs through a varied diet containing suitable calcium sources, and a supplement is not routinely necessary. However, adequacy depends on the foods offered, their portions and how much the child consumes. The conversation is different for a child who genuinely can''t get enough from food — significant dairy avoidance from a cow''s milk allergy, a vegan diet without deliberate planning, or another very limited diet. If dairy and fortified alternatives are absent, the child eats a very limited range of foods, or growth and bone health are concerns, review the overall diet with the child''s clinician before starting a supplement. More isn''t better once needs are met, either — going well beyond the reference through food alone is genuinely difficult, but stacking a supplement on top of an already-adequate diet doesn''t add benefit and isn''t something to do without medical guidance.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with a mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a moong dal and soft-cooked green beans", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed very ripe guava pulp, softened and thoroughly deseeded", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of pepper", "dinner_a": "Rice with a sambar and soft-cooked brinjal", "dinner_b": "Rice with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked brinjal", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A gentle habit, not a rule", "notes": "There''s no need to track calcium separately from the rest of your toddler''s meals. Vitamin D helps the body absorb and use calcium, but ordinary food may not provide enough vitamin D on its own — continue any vitamin D supplementation or preventive guidance already recommended for your child''s age and circumstances, and don''t change the dose on your own. A varied diet containing suitable calcium sources can meet the calcium side, depending on the foods and portions the child actually consumes. Milk, curd, paneer, calcium-set tofu, fortified alternatives, ragi, finely ground sesame and selected greens can all contribute to calcium intake without requiring daily gram-counting.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with a mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked bhindi", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bhindi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of dal with finely ground sesame stirred in, only if already introduced safely", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of dal with grated paneer and a pinch of cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and soft-cooked lauki", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked lauki", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, did calcium show up through ordinary foods like milk, curd, ragi or greens without your having to plan around it specially?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Calcium and vitamin D work together, but food alone isn''t a reliable vitamin D source. Steady, varied eating with suitable calcium sources supports the calcium side, while vitamin D still depends on the supplementation or preventive guidance appropriate for your child.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
